    {
      "@id": "item/compose-secret",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 48,
      "name": "Remove the known-value secret from compose.yaml",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/28",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "compose.yaml contains no occurrence of doccum-secret -- it appears three times today, at the x-app environment anchor (AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY) and twice inside the storage profile (MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD, and the mc alias line in minio-init) -- and the storage profile REFUSES TO RESOLVE without MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD supplied, via compose's own ${VAR:?message} form rather than a default. The original third clause, 'the default stack still boots under the smoke job', is struck: no CI job boots compose at all -- tests.yml's image job drives a single `docker run` container -- so the clause named a check that does not exist and could never be met or failed. It was also ambiguous about which stack it meant: with no profiles selected the default stack has no minio at all, since minio and minio-init are both profiles: [storage], so 'the default stack' and 'the thing the removed secret belongs to' are not the same set of services. See decision/0078. Verified instead by `docker compose config`, which resolves variables and interpolation WITHOUT starting anything: with the storage profile selected and MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD unset it must exit non-zero naming that variable, and with it set it must exit zero and its rendered output must contain no doccum-secret. That is a real check of exactly the property the item is about, it costs seconds, and it needs no running daemon beyond the compose CLI. MUTATION-CHECKED per decision/0067, and the mutation is the point rather than a formality: restoring a default -- ${MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD:-doccum-secret} instead of :? -- must make the unset case exit ZERO, which is the precise failure mode a hand-written grep for 'doccum-secret' would pass while the stack silently kept a known password. Actually booting the compose stack in CI is separate work and is NOT in this item: see item/compose-boot-ci.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T17:05:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T17:31:50Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/222"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/version-from-tag",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 49,
      "name": "The version pill reports the released tag",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/29",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "An image built from tag vX.Y.Z reports vX.Y.Z, and the development default BECOMES 0.1.0-dev. The original clause said the development default 'stays' 0.1.0-dev, which presumed a starting state that is false -- config/doccum.php line 6 reads env('DOCCUM_VERSION', '0.1.0') today, with no -dev suffix anywhere. As written the clause described a property already held and so could be reported met by changing nothing; 'becomes' is what was meant. Restated here, before the work, per decision/0068. The version pill is ALSO not yet worth asserting against, and that is the larger half of this item: nothing in the image records which tag built it, so a pill check can only confirm the app can read its own config back. Done therefore also requires the Dockerfile to take an ARG DOCCUM_VERSION and set org.opencontainers.image.version on the image, release.yml to pass the tag into it, and the container smoke to compare the rendered pill against the label read off the image -- two independent sources that a single wrong value cannot satisfy at once. MUTATION-CHECKED per decision/0067: building with one version and labelling with another must make that comparison fail. Note for whoever takes it -- release.yml has never run (git tag is empty, decision/0075), so the tag path is unexercised; prove it with a throwaway pre-release tag rather than assuming, and record the run.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T17:40:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T17:53:36Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/225"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/changelog-release-notes",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 50,
      "name": "A changelog that becomes the release notes",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/31",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "A CHANGELOG.md exists, follows one stated format, and release.yml turns the section for the tag being released into that release's notes. TWO FACTS THE ORIGINAL CLAUSE ASSUMED AND THE REPOSITORY DOES NOT HAVE. There is no CHANGELOG.md at all. And release.yml CREATES NO GITHUB RELEASE -- it logs in to ghcr, runs docker/metadata-action and docker/build-push-action, and stops; there is no softprops/action-gh-release, no `gh release create`, nothing that produces a Release object for a body to be attached to. 'Pushing a tag produces a GitHub Release whose body is that version's changelog section' therefore described machinery that does not exist, which is why it is restated here, before the work, per decision/0068. WHAT THIS ITEM CAN PROVE, and must: the extraction is a real function with real inputs, so it is testable without pushing anything. Done when a committed CHANGELOG.md carries at least an Unreleased section and a 0.1.0 section; when a small script (or equivalent step) extracts exactly one version's section by name; when release.yml calls it and passes the result as the release body; and when CI exercises the extractor directly against the committed file for a version that exists and one that does not. THE FAILURE MODE THIS IS REALLY GUARDING, because it is silent: an extractor that finds no matching section emits an EMPTY body and the release publishes successfully with blank notes. Nobody notices until a user reads the release page. So the extractor MUST exit non-zero on a missing or empty section rather than returning empty, and the CI check must assert that, not merely assert the happy path. MUTATION per decision/0067: make the extractor return empty instead of failing, and the check must go red. An extractor whose only test is the section that exists proves nothing about the case that will actually happen -- somebody tagging a version they forgot to write notes for. WHAT THIS ITEM CANNOT PROVE, and does not claim: that pushing a real tag produces a real Release with that body. release.yml has never run (git tag is empty, decision/0075), and no check here starts it. That end-to-end proof belongs to item/release-v0-1-0, which cuts a throwaway pre-release tag -- the same place the ungated-pipeline fix, the arm64 boot and item/version-from-tag's tag-to-label link all land. See decision/0081.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T18:00:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T18:29:39Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/228"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/release-gate",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 51,
      "name": "release.yml must not be able to publish from a red tree",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/32",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "release.yml has NO `needs:` on any test job and fires on workflow_dispatch as well as a tag push, so a dispatch against a red main builds and pushes `latest` to ghcr. That is a live violation of 'CI is the gate' sitting in the repository today, independent of any item. SPLIT OUT OF item/release-v0-1-0 and placed BEFORE it deliberately: the failure mode is publishing from a broken tree, and a fix for that must not be validated BY publishing from one. Landing the gate first also means the pre-release tag item/release-v0-1-0 cuts is the first thing the gate ever guards, rather than the first thing to discover the gate is wrong. Done when tests.yml gains an `on: workflow_call:` trigger -- it has none today, so this is real work and not a one-line `needs:` -- release.yml runs it as a `verify` job with the image job `needs: verify`, and the tagged commit is asserted to be an ancestor of main (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`). workflow_dispatch stays; the gate now covers it too. Re-running the reusable workflow is preferred over reading main's last run through the Actions API: that lookup inherits every pitfall decision/0016 documents -- cancelled concurrency runs, checks from other refs, pooled conclusions -- and a release should verify THE EXACT TREE IT PUBLISHES, not one that resembles it. The extra minutes per tag are the cheapest insurance in the project. MUTATION per decision/0067: tag a branch commit carrying a deliberately failing test; the release run must stop at `verify` and publish nothing. Record that run's URL. A gate nobody has watched refuse is not a gate -- and note this is the FIRST time release.yml will have run at all (decision/0075). While in this file: release.yml still pins actions/checkout@v7, docker/setup-qemu-action@v3, docker/setup-buildx-action@v3 and docker/login-action@v4 by TAG, where tests.yml pins by commit SHA. Bring them to the SHA convention, and resolve each with `git ls-remote --tags <repo> | grep '\\^{}'` -- decision/0082 records why the first line ls-remote prints is not always the commit. STATUS AS AT 2026-09-18, kept for the reasoning and superseded by the COMPLETED note at the end of this clause: the code was merged (PR #231, merge 8fdfb50) and green, and the item was deliberately NOT Completed. Its mutation has not been run and CANNOT be run from the automation environment: pushing a tag returns HTTP 403. Probed rather than assumed -- creating and updating a branch works, deleting a ref is 403, creating a tag is 403, and the agent proxy reports healthy with no relay failures, so this is a policy refusal and not a network fault. By this project's own standard a gate whose refusal nobody has watched is not proven (decision/0012, decision/0067), and marking it Completed while citing a mutation that never ran is precisely decision/0080's defect. It stays Active until the mutation runs. THE MUTATION IS READY AND ISOLATED: tag 9e2f7af, a real historical commit that IS an ancestor of main and whose image check was red (both verified) -- ancestry-check passes, verify fails, image is skipped, nothing publishes. A second tag on any non-main commit isolates ancestry-check. See decision/0085 for why this blocks the loop's own stopping condition.\n\nCORRECTED 2026-09-18 by decision/0090, and the correction is URGENT because the previous text asked a human to do the exact thing this item exists to prevent. decision/0085 said to prove the gate by tagging 9e2f7af -- 'ancestry-check passes, verify fails, image is skipped and nothing publishes'. Every one of those job names exists only in the POST-gate release.yml. GitHub runs the workflow file from the TAGGED COMMIT, and 9e2f7af predates the gate merge 8fdfb50 (verified: git merge-base --is-ancestor 8fdfb50 9e2f7af is false). git show 9e2f7af:.github/workflows/release.yml is the pre-gate file: one image job, NO needs: on anything, push: true. Tagging it would build and publish to ghcr from a red tree, under a tag this environment cannot delete (403) and a package version GITHUB_TOKEN cannot delete. DO NOT TAG 9e2f7af.\n\nWhat can honestly be proven, and what cannot. The verify->image edge cannot be mutated by a tag at all: that needs a commit which is on main, carries the gate, and fails verify, and no such commit exists -- creating one would mean pushing a red main. What a tag CAN prove is the ancestry-check refusal: tag a commit that CARRIES the gate but is not on main, and ancestry-check fails, verify is skipped, and nothing builds. That is a real refusal of a real publish attempt and it is still one tag push, so it stays behind decision/0085's wall -- but it is safe to hand a human, which the old plan was not.\n\nSeparately, never workflow_dispatch release.yml on main: metadata-action carries type=raw,value=latest,enable={{is_default_branch}} (the `image` job's `docker/metadata-action` `tags:` list), so a green dispatch on the default branch publishes the latest tag with DOCCUM_VERSION=latest -- an unversioned publish nothing asked for.\n\nCOMPLETED 2026-09-19 by mutation/0030, and by a dispatch rather than a tag. The wall recorded in decision/0085 was narrower than it was written: release.yml fires on workflow_dispatch as well as on a tag push, and a dispatch creates no ref, so the 403 that blocks `git push` of a tag does not block this. decision/0092 records that, and records that the tag wall still stands for item/release-v0-1-0 and item/tag-v1-0-0, which need a tag to EXIST rather than a workflow to run.\n\nWhat was watched: release.yml dispatched against branch mutation/0030-release-gate-ancestry, a commit carrying the gate and deliberately not on main. ancestry-check FAILED in 5 seconds with the error it was written to print, and verify, image, release and release-skipped-notice were all skipped -- so nothing was verified, built or published. Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35430359198, run_number 1: the first execution of release.yml in this repository's history, as decision/0075 said this item would be.\n\nWhat was NOT watched, and cannot be: ancestry-check passing while verify RUNS AND FAILS. That needs a commit on main, carrying the gate, that is red. None exists (40 completed tests.yml runs on main listed; two cancelled, zero failed) and manufacturing one means pushing a red main, which is what the gate is for. The Completion therefore rests on the refusal that was observed plus the observed propagation of a non-success down the same needs-chain, and says so rather than implying the whole workflow was mutated.\n\nEVERY `release.yml:NNN` CITATION IN THIS FIELD IS REPOINTED, 2026-09-21, to an anchor that moves with the thing it names (issue #371, decision/0129). The line numbers were all wrong -- not by one, but by seventy-odd, because jobs were inserted above them by pull requests that never opened this file. The claims themselves were re-verified by locating each anchor by CONTENT before the swap, so what changed is the address and not the assertion.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T18:44:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-19T07:49:31Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/231"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/release-v0-1-0",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 52,
      "name": "Tag v0.1.0 to prove the release pipeline",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/32",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "M",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/changelog-release-notes",
        "item/compose-secret",
        "item/version-from-tag",
        "item/release-gate"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "Its original clause -- 'docker pull of the 0.1.0 image works on amd64 and arm64 and boots to the installer' -- is CIRCULAR as written: pulling the published image requires the release to have already run, which is the thing being verified. The route out is a throwaway PRE-RELEASE tag (v0.1.0-rc1 or similar): cut it, let the pipeline run end to end, verify against what it actually published, then delete the tag and its release. RESTATED ALSO BECAUSE THE ITEM WAS CARRYING DEBTS ITS OWN CLAUSE NEVER NAMED. decisions 0080 and 0081 assign three proofs here that the text did not mention, which is exactly the drift between a decision and an item that leaves a requirement unowned. Named explicitly now: (1) ARM64, ACTUALLY BOOTED. release.yml publishes linux/amd64,linux/arm64 and tests.yml's image job builds and boots amd64 ONLY -- no arm64 binary has ever been executed in this project (decision/0076). Pull and boot the published image on both: amd64 on ubuntu-latest, arm64 NATIVELY on ubuntu-24.04-arm, which is free here because the repository is public. QEMU is rejected: a failure under emulation is ambiguous between emulation bug and image bug and so reads in neither direction. If the native runner turns out unavailable, DROP linux/arm64 from release.yml and strike the claim from here and item/tag-v1-0-0 rather than shipping a manifest nobody has booted. (2) THE TAG REACHES THE IMAGE. item/version-from-tag wired ARG DOCCUM_VERSION through to an org.opencontainers.image.version label, but the label and config('doccum.version') both derive from that one ARG, so the smoke's three-way check agrees with itself at a false value if the build arg is wrong (decision/0080). The only independent witness is the git ref the release ran from. Assert here that the published image's label equals the tag that triggered the run. (3) THE RELEASE CARRIES THE CHANGELOG. item/changelog-release-notes built the extractor and wired release.yml to create a Release, and nothing has ever created one (decision/0081). Assert the pre-release tag produced a Release whose body is that version's section. Sized M, not S, because that is three independent proofs plus a first-ever pipeline run; see decision/0083. The gate is NOT here -- it is item/release-gate, which lands first. ADDED AFTER CONSULTATION, because proof (3) as written CANNOT PASS: changelog-section.sh exits 1 for a pre-release tag -- verified, 'no \\'## [0.1.0-rc1]\\' section found' -- so on an rc tag `image` pushes and `release` then fails, publishing an image and creating no Release. item/changelog-release-notes' guard blocks this item's own plan. RESOLVED HERE rather than left for the run to discover: the extractor maps X.Y.Z-<suffix> to the [X.Y.Z] section and the Release is created with prerelease: true. That is the behaviour every future rc needs anyway; inventing a throwaway [0.1.0-rc1] section would put a lie in the changelog. Because the guard changes, mutation/0023 is RE-RUN, not assumed to still hold. The pre-release suffix is MANDATORY, not 'or similar': under docker/metadata-action's default flavor latest=auto a bare v0.1.0 would move the `latest` tag, and this rc must not. The amd64/arm64 boot jobs live in release.yml with needs: image -- named here because item/tag-v1-0-0's clause presumes they exist and nothing else creates them. And deleting the rc tag and its Release does NOT delete the ghcr package version, which GITHUB_TOKEN cannot do: either the rc image stays published, which the README must be able to live with, or a human deletes it -- write down which, as the manual step it is.\nONE OBSTACLE CLOSED, 2026-09-19, and the item stays Potential. PR #282 landed the part of this clause that reads \"RESOLVED HERE rather than left for the run to discover\": changelog-section.sh now tries the exact version and falls back to the release core, so v0.1.0-rc1 uses \"## [0.1.0]\", and release.yml passes prerelease: true for any tag carrying a \"-\". Two CI cases assert the extracted BODY rather than only the exit code, and three mutations were run locally and restored byte-identical (mutation/0033, 0034, 0035). The rc tag will no longer push an image and then fail to create a Release.\n\nThis closes an obstacle, not the item. Everything else in the clause above still needs the tag: the arm64 boot, the label-equals-the-tag witness, and the Release-carries-the-changelog proof are all statements about a run that has never happened.\n\nA PREMISE IN THIS CLAUSE IS FALSE FOR THIS REPOSITORY, checked against docker/metadata-action's README at the SHA release.yml pins rather than recalled. The text above says the pre-release suffix is mandatory because \"under docker/metadata-action's default flavor latest=auto a bare v0.1.0 would move the `latest` tag\". This repository does not use latest=auto: release.yml declares `type=raw,value=latest,enable={{is_default_branch}}`, which is false for every tag push, so no tag push moves `latest` at all. And for a pre-release the action degrades {{major}}.{{minor}} to {{version}} -- its README, verbatim: \"Pre-release (rc, beta, alpha) will only extend {{version}} (or {{raw}} if specified) as tag\" -- so v0.1.0-rc1 publishes 0.1.0-rc1 and nothing else, moving neither `0.1` nor `latest`.\n\nTHE CONCLUSION SURVIVES, for a different reason, and the difference matters to whoever cuts the tag. The suffix is mandatory because a bare v0.1.0 would BE the release: it would publish the stable container tags, create a real GitHub Release from the [0.1.0] notes, and there would be nothing throwaway about it. The container-tag hazard the clause names is not the one to watch. See decision/0103.\n\nBUILT, 2026-09-20, in PR #287: boot-published-image (matrix amd64 on ubuntu-latest, arm64 NATIVELY on ubuntu-24.04-arm, needs: image, no QEMU), verify-version-label (needs: image; resolves the expected version from GITHUB_REF_NAME independently of the image job's metadata-action output, so the comparison is against the git ref rather than against a second reader of the same build ARG), and verify-release-notes (needs: release; compares the GitHub Release body against a fresh changelog-section.sh extraction, both sides normalised for CRLF because the Releases API returns CRLF for notes posted with LF). All three are guarded `if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/')`.\n\nTHIS PARAGRAPH PREVIOUSLY READ 'STILL NOT BUILT, and deliberately', and that is kept here rather than overwritten because the reasoning was wrong, not merely out of date. It argued that landing the jobs would 'add workflow code nobody has watched execute -- the substitution decision/0087 holds item/image-size open for'. decision/0087 is about marking something COMPLETED on the strength of an unexercised step; writing the step marks nothing. The cost ran the other way: if the rc were cut while these were absent, the rc would prove none of the three things it exists to prove and a second tag would be needed.\n\nTHE CLAUSE OUTLIVED THE PR THAT FALSIFIED IT BY A DAY. The correction was written -- in #287's merge commit message and in the PR node's name -- but not into the clause, which is the text decision/0083 put operative wording in doneWhen to make binding. Nothing in CI can catch this: the validator checks the ledger's shape and its graph, never whether a clause's prose describes the repository. See decision/0106.\n\nWHAT STILL NEEDS THE TAG is unchanged and is now the whole of the item: none of these three jobs has ever run, because every one of them is gated on a tag push and no tag exists. The arm64 boot, the label-equals-the-tag witness and the Release-carries-the-changelog proof are still statements about a run that has not happened. The item stays Potential.\n\nTHE rc-IMAGE DISPOSAL CLAUSE IS DISCHARGED, 2026-09-21, in PR #343 (issue #341). It said: \"either the rc image stays published, which the README must be able to live with, or a human deletes it -- write down which, as the manual step it is\". Nothing had, and a grep over README.md, HANDOVER.md, CONTRIBUTING.md and docs/self-hosting/ for 'package version', 'rc image' and 'stays published' returned empty; the observation existed only in issue #302's body, a discussion thread rather than somewhere a person cutting a release looks.\n\nTHE CHOICE: THE rc IMAGE STAYS, written into HANDOVER.md's \"Before a public release\" step 2. The load-bearing half of that choice is that a bare `docker pull ghcr.io/turbophp/doccum` cannot reach it, and it was verified rather than assumed: the `image` job's `type=raw,value=latest,enable=${{ ... !contains(github.ref_name, '-') }}` entry gates `latest` on it, and `-` is SemVer's pre-release separator, so no pre-release tag moves the tag the README's quick start pulls. The delete path is written down too, with the fact that makes it a manual step: GITHUB_TOKEN cannot delete a ghcr package version, so no workflow can clean up after itself and there is no automation to wait for.\n\nA SECOND, SMALLER STALENESS FIXED IN THE SAME STEP. It read \"Tag v0.1.0\" flatly. Under this item's own plan the first tag is a pre-release and the suffix is MANDATORY -- docker/metadata-action defaults to latest=auto, so a bare v0.1.0 would move `latest`, which a rehearsal must not do. release.yml carries no `flavor:` block, checked rather than inferred from this item's prose. Under issue #302's other options a real v0.1.0 IS the right first tag, so the step now names neither and points at #302. Nothing here decides #302.\n\nTHIS doneWhen CONTRADICTED ITSELF, AND THE LATER PARAGRAPH IS THE CORRECT ONE, 2026-09-21, issue #348. Two paragraphs above assert opposite things about the same workflow. The decision/0103 paragraph says \"This repository does not use latest=auto: release.yml declares type=raw,value=latest,enable={{is_default_branch}}, which is false for every tag push, so NO TAG PUSH MOVES `latest` AT ALL\". The last paragraph, written a day later in PR #343, says \"docker/metadata-action defaults to latest=auto, so a bare v0.1.0 would move `latest` ... release.yml carries no `flavor:` block, checked rather than inferred\". Both are in the field a person cutting the first tag reads, and a reader who stops at the first comes away with the opposite model of the pipeline from one who reads to the end.\n\nTHE LAST PARAGRAPH IS RIGHT. Verified at the pinned SHA and recorded in full under item/tag-v1-0-0's clause (3): with no `flavor:` block the `latest` option is at its default `auto`, which generates `latest` for `type=semver,pattern=...`, and the `image` job's `tags:` list carries exactly that entry. So a bare v0.1.0 or v1.0.0 tag DOES publish `latest`. The decision/0103 paragraph is left standing, as the ledger's history always is, but it may not be read alone.\n\nTHE OPERATIVE CONCLUSION OF BOTH PARAGRAPHS IS UNCHANGED: the pre-release suffix is mandatory. Under the correct reading it is mandatory for the stronger reason -- a bare v0.1.0 would move `latest`, which is the tag every quick start in this repository pulls, in addition to being a real release rather than a rehearsal. AND THE rc IS SAFE, now from source rather than by inference: procSemver's prerelease branch leaves latest false, and the `type=raw,value=latest,enable=${{ ... }}` entry independently excludes any github.ref_name containing `-`. Both mechanisms agree, so the rehearsal tag cannot move `latest`.\n\nTHE SENTENCE ABOVE IS OUT OF DATE, 2026-09-21, and is corrected here rather than deleted so the reasoning that produced it stays legible (decision/0129). The citation \"release.yml:131\" pointed at the `type=raw,value=latest,enable=${{ ... }}` entry in the `image` job's `tags:` list, and by the time this was written the line was 207 (a number quoted as history, not as an address -- naming a fresh one here would rot the same way). The rule it names is unchanged and still reads `type=raw,value=latest,enable=${{ startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/') && !contains(github.ref_name, '-') }}`, so the CLAIM holds and only its address rotted. It was moved by PR #359, which inserted the changelog-section job above it and never opened this file. That is the half of decision/0129 nobody can be careless about: a line number is a pointer into a document that grows, and an insertion anywhere above it invalidates it silently.\n\nEVERY `release.yml:NNN` CITATION IN THIS FIELD IS REPOINTED, 2026-09-21, to an anchor that moves with the thing it names (issue #371, decision/0129). The line numbers were all wrong -- not by one, but by seventy-odd, because jobs were inserted above them by pull requests that never opened this file. The claims themselves were re-verified by locating each anchor by CONTENT before the swap, so what changed is the address and not the assertion.",
