    },
    {
      "@id": "item/docs-site",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 60,
      "name": "Publish the docs to GitHub Pages",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/27",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/self-hosting-docs"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "The Pages workflow is green and the README links to the published site.\n\nSTATUS 2026-09-19: Active, and the split is worth stating because this item was previously reported to the owner as fully blocked on a repository setting. It was not. The repository had NO Pages workflow at all -- checked, .github/workflows held format, ledger, phpstan-baseline, release, security and tests -- so the generator, the workflow, a green build job and the test that every self-hosting page is reachable from the index were all buildable without anyone's permission, and shipped in PR #274. Reading \"this item ends at something I cannot authorise\" as \"nothing in this item can be built\" is the error; see decision/0099.\n\nWHAT REMAINS is exactly two settings and the clauses they unblock: Settings -> Pages -> Source: GitHub Actions, then a repository variable DOCS_SITE_PAGES_ENABLED = true. The deploy job is gated on that variable so it SKIPS rather than fails until both are done -- verified on main at fa499f5, where the pages workflow concluded success with build green and deploy skipped. Once set, deploy publishes, and the README link (deliberately NOT added while the URL 404s) closes the second clause.\n\nTHE decision/0099 CITATION ABOVE IS FALSE and is corrected rather than deleted, 2026-09-21. decision/0099's subject is a job gated only on the branch, invisible to the pull request whose merge it will break; its rationale contains neither \"authorise\" nor \"buildable\" (grep: 0). The error this clause describes -- reading \"this item ends at something I cannot authorise\" as \"nothing in this item can be built\" -- is decision/0107's, and was attributed to 0099 in three places at once. See decision/0110.\n\nTHE INDEX PAGE'S FIRST PARAGRAPH WAS FALSE, fixed 2026-09-21 in PR #360. docs/self-hosting/README.md said \"see the generated OpenAPI reference at /docs/api\"; decision/0118 is titled \"The contract test supersedes Scramble, and /docs/api is retired\", and `grep -rn \"docs/api\" routes/` returns nothing. build-docs-site.mjs:52 makes that file the published site's index, so the first sentence a reader met on the site would have been false the moment the two settings below are flipped -- a defect that would have shipped WITH the clause it is waiting on, rather than being caught after.\n\nIt now names docs/api/openapi.json, and deliberately not as a markdown link: resolveHref() leaves an unknown target alone so that a broken link stays visibly broken, and the site publishes only the nine self-hosting pages.\n\nTHE TWO SETTINGS ARE UNCHANGED and remain the whole of what this item waits on.",
      "actionStatus": "ActiveActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-19T13:30:00Z",
      "endTime": null,
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/274"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/image-size",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 61,
      "name": "Measure and reduce the image",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/30",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "M",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "The release job prints the image's compressed and uncompressed size, and the README states both. RESTATED under decision/0087: the clause used to end 'and the image is at or below its stated budget', which names a budget that does not exist -- no number is stated anywhere in this repository, so the clause was unfalsifiable and could be called met by measuring anything at all. Inventing a number now would be a guess dressed as a requirement, which is the failure decision/0068 was written against. The budget is therefore DEFINED AS the size this item measures on its own first run, recorded in the README and in the Completed item's record as two numbers with the commit they were measured at. After that it is a ratchet: any later change that pushes either number above the recorded figure must carry a Decision saying why, or be cut. Done when both numbers are printed by the release job, both are in the README with the commit they came from, and the ratchet is enforced by something CI runs rather than by the README asking politely -- a check that reads the built image's size and fails above the recorded figure. A README sentence alone is documentation of an intention, not a gate, and this item has no other guard.\n\nSTATUS, 2026-09-18: Active, NOT Completed, and the difference is one clause. The measurement, the record and the ratchet all shipped in PR #244 and the ratchet is mutation-proven (mutation/0026: image was the only failing check of fifteen, both error lines, exit 1). What has NOT happened is 'printed by the release job'. release.yml has never run -- decision/0085's tag wall, the same thing holding item/release-gate open -- so the print step added to it is code nobody has watched execute. Marking this Completed would count an unexercised workflow step as done because the rest of the item is, which is the exact substitution decision/0087 was written to stop. It completes when a tag push happens and that job's log shows the two numbers.\n\nAMENDED by decision/0089, and recorded here because the clause above is the binding one: decision/0083 put operative clauses in doneWhen precisely so a Decision's prose is not the thing anyone has to re-read. The check does NOT fail above the recorded figure. It fails above the recorded figure PLUS a stated tolerance, currently 2%, kept in .github/image-budget.json. The tolerance is the measurement's noise floor, not slack: two builds of identical source measured 979591435 then 979591488 uncompressed (+53 bytes) and 308438108 then 308434885 compressed (-3223), so a byte-exact ratchet fails on the next build for something this repository did not do, and the opposite directions rule out recording the larger of two builds. Writing decision/0089 and leaving this clause reading 'fails above the recorded figure' left the ledger asserting something the code does not do -- caught within a day, by a consultation rather than by the validator.\n\nSTILL ACTIVE 2026-09-19, and NOT for the reason previously recorded. The clause holding it open is 'printed by the release job', which needs the `image` job to run; decision/0085's tag wall was named as the blocker. mutation/0030 showed that wall does not cover workflow_dispatch, so release.yml can now be run on demand -- and this item is still not done, because reaching `image` means reaching a successful publish. A dispatch on main would publish ghcr `latest` with DOCCUM_VERSION=latest (the `image` job passes `DOCCUM_VERSION=${{ steps.meta.outputs.version }}` as a build arg), the unversioned publish decision/0090 warned against; a dispatch on a branch that IS on main reaches `image` with no matching tag rule at all. The first is the owner's call, not the loop's. See decision/0092, which also flags that the second is a reading of release.yml and metadata-action's documented behaviour rather than something measured.\n\nEVERY `release.yml:NNN` CITATION IN THIS FIELD IS REPOINTED, 2026-09-21, to an anchor that moves with the thing it names (issue #371, decision/0129). The line numbers were all wrong -- not by one, but by seventy-odd, because jobs were inserted above them by pull requests that never opened this file. The claims themselves were re-verified by locating each anchor by CONTENT before the swap, so what changed is the address and not the assertion.",
      "actionStatus": "ActiveActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T20:00:00Z",
      "endTime": null,
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/244"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/readme-owner",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 62,
      "name": "A README that describes the shipped product",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/33",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/docs-site",
        "item/image-size",
        "item/release-v0-1-0"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "README contains no OWNER placeholder and its quick start uses the published image.\n\nSPLIT, 2026-09-20, because the two clauses have different blockers and only one of them ever needed this item's dependencies. CLAUSE 1 -- 'README contains no OWNER placeholder' -- IS DISCHARGED, in PR #296. CLAUSE 2 -- 'its quick start uses the published image' -- still needs a published image, which is item/release-v0-1-0's, so the item stays Potential.\n\nTHE ITEM WAS GATED ON THE WRONG CLAUSE. dependsOn names item/docs-site, item/image-size and item/release-v0-1-0; all three are about clause 2. Nothing about clause 1 needed any of them, and it sat unbuilt for four days behind them. That is decision/0099's error repeating -- reading 'this item ends at something I cannot authorise' as 'nothing in this item can be built' -- and it is the third instance in one session, which is what decision/0107 records.\n\nTHE PLACEHOLDER WAS IN FIVE PLACES, not the one the clause names: README.md, the three docs/self-hosting pages the docs site publishes (quick-start, upgrading, backup-and-restore), and a HANDOVER note about it. Meanwhile release.yml has always pushed to ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }} and two of its own comments already spell out ghcr.io/turbophp/doccum, so the project knew the owner and asked every reader to supply it. Owner verified from `git remote get-url origin` and release.yml's `images: ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}`, not recalled.\n\nA CI JOB CARRIES THE RATCHET -- `docs name the real image, not a placeholder` -- because item/image-size's clause already made the general argument: a README sentence alone is documentation of an intention, not a gate. It was mutation-checked, and the first version was broken in a way worth recording: its search string and its error message both contain the literal placeholder, so it matched its own job body and would have been red on every run forever. Caught by running the grep locally before pushing, fixed with a pathspec exclusion whose cost is stated in the job's comment, then re-run clean both directions. A second step asserts the grep's corpus is non-empty and still holds the four pages, because a grep that searches nothing passes forever.\n\nTHE decision/0099 CITATION ABOVE IS FALSE and is corrected rather than deleted, 2026-09-21. decision/0099's subject is a job gated only on the branch, invisible to the pull request whose merge it will break; its rationale contains neither \"authorise\" nor \"buildable\" (grep: 0). The error this clause describes -- reading \"this item ends at something I cannot authorise\" as \"nothing in this item can be built\" -- is decision/0107's, and was attributed to 0099 in three places at once. See decision/0110.\n\nCLAUSE 2 CANNOT BE SATISFIED BEFORE A STABLE TAG, and the plan as written routes around that in a circle. Verified at 6a3e906, not inferred. README.md's quick-start block is `docker run ... ghcr.io/turbophp/doccum` with no tag, so the quick start resolves `:latest`. release.yml declares `latest` twice: line 130 `enable={{is_default_branch}}`, false on every tag push, and line 131 `enable=${{ startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/') && !contains(github.ref_name, '-') }}`, false for a pre-release. So `latest` does not exist until a NON-PRE-RELEASE tag is pushed. Then: this clause needs `latest`; `latest` needs a stable tag; the stable tag is item/tag-v1-0-0, which dependsOn item/v1-audit, which dependsOn THIS ITEM. The dependsOn graph is acyclic and the clause semantics are not -- the same shape decision/0083 found in item/release-v0-1-0's original clause, one item over.\n\nTHE rc DOES NOT BREAK THE CYCLE. v0.1.0-rc1 publishes 0.1.0-rc1 and nothing else, so it moves no `latest` and leaves this clause exactly where it is. It also leaves `verify-latest-resolves-to-the-tag` (PR #295) unexecuted, because that job is guarded on a non-pre-release tag by design -- so the `latest` publish and its digest assertion would both run for the first time at v1.0.0, the one tag that cannot be retried.\n\nNOT RESOLVED HERE, because the way out is a release decision. Cutting a real v0.1.0 rather than only an rc would publish `latest`, exercise every tag-gated job, satisfy this clause and give item/v1-audit a published image -- and would make 0.1.0 public, which HANDOVER.md's \"Before a public release\" step 2 already contemplates ('Tag v0.1.0'). That is the owner's appetite to judge, not the loop's, and it is filed as an issue rather than left in run prose. See decision/0110.\n\nTHE SENTENCE ABOVE IS OUT OF DATE, 2026-09-21, and is corrected here rather than deleted so the reasoning that produced it stays legible (decision/0129). Since PR #366 the rc does NOT leave verify-latest-resolves-to-the-tag unexecuted: the job runs on any tag and short-circuits inside itself on a pre-release, so on an rc it goes green having compared no digests. The paragraph's CONCLUSION is unchanged and is the reason this correction matters rather than being bookkeeping -- the `latest` publish and its digest assertion still both happen for the first time at the first non-pre-release tag, the one tag that cannot be retried. What changed is only that the evidence now looks like a green check instead of a skipped one, which is a worse-looking green and was accepted as the price of not skipping `release`.\n\nEVERY `release.yml:NNN` CITATION IN THIS FIELD IS REPOINTED, 2026-09-21, to an anchor that moves with the thing it names (issue #371, decision/0129). The line numbers were all wrong -- not by one, but by seventy-odd, because jobs were inserted above them by pull requests that never opened this file. The claims themselves were re-verified by locating each anchor by CONTENT before the swap, so what changed is the address and not the assertion.\n\nA LINE-NUMBER CITATION IN THIS FIELD IS REPOINTED, 2026-09-22, onto an anchor that moves with the thing it names (issue #371). It had rotted -- the cited line now holds something else -- and this field is operative text, read for what to do now, so unlike a Decision's rationale it is maintained rather than preserved. See item/citation-addresses-rot.",
      "actionStatus": "PotentialActionStatus",
      "startTime": null,
      "endTime": null,
      "result": []
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/smoke-installer-names-the-bounce",
      "@type": "Action",
