    {
      "@id": "item/directory-force-delete-orphans",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 64,
      "name": "Force-deleting a directory must not orphan the objects beneath it",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/86",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:6-storage-and-period-partitioning"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "files.directory_id is constrained with cascadeOnDelete, so forceDelete() on a Directory removes every descendant File ROW in SQL without PHP ever running per file -- so Services\\DocumentStorage is never reached and every object those files owned is left in the store with nothing referencing it. Directory::booted()'s forceDeleted hook clears only the directory's own morph-attached properties; it cannot clean up descendants the database already removed. LATENT, not live: nothing in the application force-deletes a Directory today, and PeriodPurger force-deletes FILES one at a time, deleting each object through DocumentStorage first -- which is exactly the ordering a cascade skips. Done when the objects beneath a force-deleted directory are removed through the DocumentStorage seam, or the cascade is replaced by a path that cannot bypass it, with a test that fails if the objects survive. The test must assert against the object store, not the rows: asserting the rows are gone passes on the broken code, since the cascade is precisely what removes them.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T19:53:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T20:08:30Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/243"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/upgrade-preserves-access",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 65,
      "name": "Upgrading an existing instance must not lock out every user",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/210",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "item/email-verification-decided made User implement MustVerifyEmail and put every content and admin route behind auth+verified, without backfilling email_verified_at. On an existing instance every pre-existing user is then refused everywhere -- the administrator included, so the in-app vouch path item/admin-users built is itself behind the gate, and with MAIL_MAILER=log the verification mail goes to a server log file. Done when an additive migration restores the access those accounts already had: preservation, not a grant, since before that change verification enforced nothing. Backfilled from created_at rather than now(), because what is true of them is that they have been trusted since they were created -- and via COALESCE(created_at, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP), because timestamps() makes created_at nullable and only Eloquent create() fills it, so a bare column reference would set an imported row to null, keep matching whereNull forever, and leave exactly that user locked out by the migration written so nobody is locked out. The test must distinguish a backfilled pre-existing row from an empty table, since RefreshDatabase runs the migration against no users. BASIS: recorded under spec:13-infrastructure rather than spec:10-user-facing-surface, and the reasoning is stated because the rule it sidesteps is a real one. The validator requires a Completed spec:10 item to carry a negative Mutation -- the smoke-proof requirement for UI work -- and this fix is a boot-time migration with a unit test, not a UI change. Its SYMPTOM is user-facing (nobody can log in); its SUBSTANCE is the upgrade seam, which CLAUDE.md and item/upgrade-smoke both treat as infrastructure. The user-facing proof is not waived, it is tracked: item/upgrade-smoke depends on this item and its doneWhen requires a mutation-checked assertion that a pre-existing user still reaches the app. If that item does not land, this fix stays unwitnessed by the smoke, and that is the thing to notice rather than the basis string.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T15:25:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T15:40:03Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/212"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/role-permissions-survive-restart",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 66,
      "name": "A role permission an operator edits must survive a container restart",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/214",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "AUTORUN_ENABLED=true (Dockerfile:68) makes docker/entrypoint.d/51-doccum-roles.sh run doccum:ensure-roles on every boot, and RolesAndPermissionsSeeder::run() calls syncPermissions() on both roles -- which REPLACES the set. So every restart, the documented upgrade procedure, discards whatever item/admin-roles's UI let an operator configure. Not inference about the package: SetRolePermissions depends on that same replace behaviour to remove a permission when an operator unticks it. Done when an edit survives a restart, while the seeder still creates missing roles and permissions on a fresh install and still makes a permission added by an upgrade EXIST as an assignable row -- those are what EnsureRoles exists for. That clause was ambiguous as first written and is settled here, before the work is judged against it: 'picks up' meant, in EnsureRoles's original docblock, that existing roles were GRANTED the new permission, because syncPermissions() granted it as a side effect of replacing the set. Only the weaker reading survives -- the Permission row is created on every boot, and existing roles are left untouched. The stronger one is given up deliberately, and is arguably not a loss: a new capability that grants ITSELF to every existing member on upgrade is a privilege-escalation path, not a convenience, and the operator granting it through Settings -> Roles is the same surface that lets them revoke one. Preserving all three properties at once -- operator grants, operator revocations, and automatic upgrade grants -- needs version-tracked deltas, which is real machinery for a problem with a one-click remedy. A release note naming any newly added permission is the cost of this choice and belongs with item/changelog-release-notes. The decision about what 'ensure' means is stated rather than implied: creating what is absent is not the same operation as asserting what the set must be, and only the first is safe to run on every boot against a database an operator can edit. The container smoke asserts it ACROSS the replacement -- runVerify() re-checks the permission runSetup() granted, which is nearly free since the grant already happens -- and restoring the destructive sync must make that assertion fail.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T16:20:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T16:54:34Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/218"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/upgrade-smoke",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 67,
      "name": "CI must exercise an upgrade, not only a restart",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/210",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "M",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/upgrade-preserves-access"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "tests.yml's 'Replace the container, keeping only the volume' step runs the SAME image tag on both sides, so it proves persistence across a restart and not an upgrade across a code change. That is why item/upgrade-preserves-access shipped: item/email-verification-decided's doneWhen asked the no-mailer question, answered it for a fresh install, and cited this smoke as proof -- correctly, for the question it actually answers. Done when CI boots a volume whose rows predate the candidate image and asserts a pre-existing user still reaches the app. The cheaper first cut is preferred over maintaining an older published image: seed the volume's SQLite directly with a row in the pre-change state (email_verified_at NULL, a role whose permissions were edited) and restart the SAME image against it, which narrows to the regression class without needing an old image whose schema drifts further from current with every migration. Mutation-checked per decision/0067: reverting the backfill migration must make the new assertion fail. item/role-permissions-survive-restart's across-the-replacement check is the second thing this stage should carry.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T20:45:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T21:16:53Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/250"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/v1-audit",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 68,
      "name": "Audit the whole surface against the spec",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/34",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/admin-instance-settings",
        "item/admin-periods",
        "item/admin-roles",
        "item/ci-db-matrix",
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/directory-access-ui",
        "item/directory-force-delete-orphans",
        "item/directory-policy-trashed-move",
        "item/directory-policy-trashed-update",
        "item/home-dashboard",
        "item/image-size",
        "item/openapi-reference",
        "item/palette-smoke",
        "item/purge-commands",
        "item/readme-owner",
        "item/search-indexer-null-subject",
        "item/smoke-installer-names-the-bounce",
        "item/snippet-escape-order",
        "item/spec-reconcile",
        "item/spec-section8-reconcile",
        "item/trash-view",
        "item/upgrade-smoke"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "A checklist in the PR body walks spec milestones 1 to 11 and this backlog; pint clean, phpstan at or below baseline, smoke green; a manual run of the published image through install, upload, share, search, trash and a purge dry run is recorded.\n\nCLAUSE (b) IS DISCHARGED, 2026-09-21, in PR #336, against main run `tests` 621 at b8b5026 -- a push run on main, not a PR run, per LOOP.md step 3. pint via the `lint` job's \"Pint (format check)\" step, phpstan via its \"PHPStan\" step, smoke via `image`.\n\nAND THE INSTRUCTION FOR SATISFYING (b) WAS UNRUNNABLE AS WRITTEN. docs/v1-audit.md's first pass said (b) was claimable \"by check name: `pint`, `lint` ... and `image`\". There is no `pint` CHECK on a main push: pint is a job of .github/workflows/format.yml, whose on.push.branches is `claude/**` and `ledger/**` and nothing else. A reader following it would hunt a main run for a check that never appears there, or count a `pint` check belonging to another ref pointing at the same commit -- which LOOP.md step 3 records having already happened, on a0cf122. Pint does run on main, as a STEP of `lint`. A plan naming the wrong check is decision/0080's defect class arriving later and more quietly: nothing rejects it until somebody tries to follow it.\n\nCLAUSE (a) WAS RE-WALKED in the same PR because milestone 11 had failed on three gaps, and all three shipped (#321, #322, #323, their issues closed). Milestone 11 now passes.\n\nTHE SAME PR RETRACTED ITS OWN CENTRAL CONCLUSION, which is the part worth carrying forward. A draft said the loop's remaining v1 work was zero. An adversarial read before it merged found FIVE clause fragments needing nobody's permission -- the README screenshot (which the audit itself had moved into the owner's column, though CI boots a container on every push), a missing CHANGELOG Security entry, this item's sibling clause in item/release-v0-1-0, clause (c)'s COVERAGE as distinct from its subject, and issue #303. Filed as issues #338, #339, #340, #341; #338, #341 and #303 have since shipped. That was the fifth instance of reading at ITEM granularity where blockers live at CLAUSE granularity -- the error decision/0116 rewrote LOOP.md step 4 to forbid, committed by the document whose job is to catch it.\n\nCLAUSE (c) REMAINS, and only its SUBJECT is owner-blocked. The published image needs a tag (#302). The EXERCISE does not: issue #340 covers adding the missing purge dry run to the container smoke and pointing the full smoke at the published image, which would make (c) discharge itself when a tag lands rather than needing a person with a terminal.\n\nCLAUSE (c)'S COVERAGE HALF IS BUILT, 2026-09-21, in PR #346, and its load-bearing assertion is proven by mutation/0051. The clause asks for a run through \"install, upload, share, search, trash AND A PURGE DRY RUN\". Five of those six had a check in .github/scripts/container-smoke.mjs; the sixth did not exist. The only \"purge\" in the file was checkTrashViewRestoreAndPurge()'s TRASH purge -- a different command over a different table, which proves nothing about doccum:purge-period. checkPurgePeriodDryRunLeavesFileInPlace() now builds a File in its own period (2019-03, not the existing check's 2020-01, so it cannot depend on that check having run first), closes the period through the real /admin/periods form, runs the real artisan command in the shipped image via docker exec WITHOUT --force, and reads the row back.\n\nTHE THREE STDOUT CHECKS ARE NOT THE PROOF, and the PR said so before the mutation rather than after. A dry run that silently purged for real would print the period label, the file count and the literal 'Dry run. Nothing was deleted. Pass --force to purge.' identically -- that string is a literal in PurgePeriod::handle(), reached or not reached. mutation/0051 confirmed exactly that: under a mutant whose dry-run branch force-deleted the files while printing every word unchanged, the three stdout assertions PASSED and the row read-back failed. CLAUDE.md's rule is that a smoke assertion is worth only what a mutation says it is; this is what it says.\n\nWHAT IS STILL NOT DONE ON (c): its SUBJECT, and the other half of issue #340. The subject needs a tag (#302) -- the exercise is meant to run against the PUBLISHED image, and pointing the full smoke at it in release.yml's boot-published-image is part 2 of #340, deliberately not in #346.\n\nMILESTONE 11'S FOURTH OBSERVATION IS DISCHARGED, 2026-09-21, in PR #351 (issue #339). It was the one gap milestone 11 still carried after its re-walk: spec 12 asks README for \"what it is, screenshot, three-line quick start\" and the screenshot did not exist. docs/images/readme-files-view.png now does, shown between the first and the third as spec 12 orders them.\n\nPRODUCED BY CI FROM THE REAL CONTAINER, which is what made this the loop's work rather than the owner's. The first pass had filed it under clause (c) reasoning that a screenshot \"needs a running instance to produce\"; the image job boots the container and drives Playwright at it on every push. The committed PNG is 1440x420, lifted byte for byte from the readme-screenshot artifact of run 35629541956.\n\nTHE CAPTURE IS A CHECK, NOT A SIDE EFFECT. It waits for the directory tree, the files table and the uploaded file's own row -- each named separately so a failure says which -- then refuses a PNG that is missing or implausibly small. It earned that immediately: the first version captured at /files, which is the ROOT, while uploadAndProveStored() puts its file one directory down, and the check refused to photograph the empty listing rather than shipping a picture of it.\n\nAND IT STILL PASSED SOMETHING UNSHIPPABLE, recorded as decision/0123. The first correct capture was two thirds empty -- a true photograph of a sparse page, and every assertion above is exactly true of it. Only opening the file found that. The staleness question spec 12 leaves open is answered in README.md rather than implied: the committed image is point-in-time, a fresh capture rides every CI run as an artifact, and a pixel-diff gate was rejected because a browser screenshot moves with font rendering and timing.\n\nCLAUSE (c)'S COVERAGE IS COMPLETE, 2026-09-21, in PRs #353 and #355 (issue #340), and only its SUBJECT is owner-blocked now. The clause asks for a run of the PUBLISHED image through install, upload, share, search, trash and a purge dry run. The sixth step landed with PR #346; PR #353 pointed the whole smoke at the PULLED image in release.yml's boot-published-image, amd64 leg. So all six steps now run against the artifact a self-hoster pulls rather than only against one this repository built, and PR #355 wrote that split into docs/v1-audit.md.\n\nWHAT IS PROVEN IS THE WIRING, NOT THE PASSAGE, and the distinction is recorded rather than blurred. boot-published-image is guarded to a tag push and no tag exists, so the job has still never executed and cannot be made to. assert-published-smoke.py parses release.yml and container-smoke.mjs on every pull request and refuses unless that job checks out, invokes the smoke, binds CONTAINER_NAME to the container its own docker run creates, binds BASE_URL to the port that docker run publishes, and supplies every env('SMOKE_...') the smoke requires with no default -- decision/0120's pattern, and the strongest evidence a tag-gated job admits. Four mutants back it, and only three are Mutation nodes: 0052, 0054 and 0055, all negative. The fourth -- a meaning-preserving reflow of the job's steps, env keys and docker run flags, which the guard had to SURVIVE -- is recorded in decision/0126 and not as a node, because a survival control is not what this vocabulary calls a Mutation. It matters as much as the three: a check that reddens on a reflow is withdrawn within a week, and the guard goes with it.\n\nTHE GUARD SHIPPED WITH THE DEFECT IT NOW CATCHES, which is decision/0124: boot-published-image had no actions/checkout at all -- it had never needed one -- and the guard's first version would have passed it while the job died on its first Node step at tag time. Caught by comparing the job against its siblings rather than against the diff.\n\nNOTHING IN CLAUSE (c) IS LEFT FOR THIS LOOP TO BUILD. One tag push remains, and it is the owner's to authorise (#302).",
