    {
      "@id": "item/tag-v1-0-0",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 69,
      "name": "Tag and publish 1.0.0",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/35",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/v1-audit"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "docker run of the 1.0.0 image boots to the installer on amd64 and arm64; the GitHub Release exists; the ledger's about.version reads 1.0.0. ADDED AFTER CONSULTATION. This clause presumes machinery nobody has built and would half-publish as written. (1) CHANGELOG.md has no [1.0.0] section -- verified -- so the tag push would push the image and THEN fail at `release`, which is exactly the half-published state item/changelog-release-notes was built to prevent, produced by its own guard. Require: a [1.0.0] section exists and the changelog-section job passes for it BEFORE the tag is pushed. (2) 'boots on amd64 and arm64' presumes the boot jobs item/release-v0-1-0 adds to release.yml; name them rather than presuming. (3) Add: `docker pull ghcr.io/turbophp/doccum` with no tag resolves to 1.0.0. The README quick start pulls latest and LOOP.md's stopping condition says ghcr carries the image; nothing currently asserts the two agree. (4) Say who updates the ledger's release node after the tag, since the loop's stopping rule reads it and this is the last item before it.\n\nAMENDED 2026-09-20 under decision/0106, which is the rule that a PR implementing part of an item amends that item's doneWhen in the ledger pass that follows. Two PRs implement parts of the clause above and neither was written into it; this item was the second one decision/0106 named in `affects`, and the amendment is made rather than merely promised.\n\nCLAUSE (2) IS DISCHARGED. It said \"'boots on amd64 and arm64' presumes the boot jobs item/release-v0-1-0 adds to release.yml; name them rather than presuming\". They exist, from PR #287, and the name is `boot-published-image` -- one job, a matrix of two: amd64 on ubuntu-latest and arm64 NATIVELY on ubuntu-24.04-arm, `needs: image`, no QEMU. Its sibling `verify-version-label` (`needs: image`) carries the label witness and `verify-release-notes` (`needs: release`) the changelog one. All three are guarded `if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/')`. So clause (2) no longer presumes anything: it requires that `boot-published-image` pass on both matrix legs for the v1.0.0 tag. Note what is NOT discharged -- none of the three has ever executed, because the guard that makes them safe also makes them untested. This item inherits that from item/release-v0-1-0 rather than repeating it: the rc proves the jobs work, and this item then re-runs them on a real tag.\n\nCLAUSE (3) IS NOW MECHANICALLY POSSIBLE, and still not asserted. It asks that `docker pull ghcr.io/turbophp/doccum` with no tag resolve to 1.0.0. A bare pull resolves `:latest`, and until PR #286 release.yml declared `latest` only as `type=raw,value=latest,enable={{is_default_branch}}` -- false for every tag push, so no tag moved `latest` at all and clause (3) was unsatisfiable by construction rather than merely unimplemented. #286 added `type=raw,value=latest,enable=${ startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/') && !contains(github.ref_name, '-') }`, so a non-pre-release tag now publishes `latest` alongside `{{version}}` while an rc publishes neither. Both lines are present and that is deliberate, not a leftover: the first covers a workflow_dispatch, the second a tag. What clause (3) still requires is the ASSERTION, which nothing performs -- that after the v1.0.0 tag, `ghcr.io/turbophp/doccum` and `ghcr.io/turbophp/doccum:1.0.0` resolve to the same digest. Comparing the two pulls' digests is the check; comparing either against the tag name would be a second reader of the same value, which is what decision/0080 was written against.\n\nCLAUSES (1) AND (4) ARE UNCHANGED, and re-verified rather than assumed. CHANGELOG.md holds `## [Unreleased]` and `## [0.1.0] - 2026-09-18` and no `[1.0.0]` section, so clause (1) stands exactly as written. Clause (4) is issue #289, which is the owner's: about.version is currently 0.1.0 and nothing says whether that names the version this ledger describes, the version published to ghcr, or the version the tree would build -- three readings that diverge precisely during a release, which is when the field is read. decision/0095 calls fixing it a vocabulary change and decision/0105 puts schema changes with the owner, so the loop states the choice rather than making it.\n\nCLAUSE (3) IS BUILT, 2026-09-20, in PR #295, one pass after the amendment above said it was 'now mechanically possible, and still not asserted'. Naming a missing assertion in a clause and not building it is what decision/0107 calls displacement; this discharges it. The job is `verify-latest-resolves-to-the-tag` (needs: image, guarded `startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/') && !contains(github.ref_name, '-')` -- narrower than its siblings because `latest` publishes only on a non-pre-release tag, so on an rc a bare pull would resolve to a stale image this run did not build).\n\nIT COMPARES DIGESTS, and the reason is the same one that shaped verify-version-label. `latest` and {{version}} are two tags pushed from ONE build, so any value read out of both images is one source wearing two hats (decision/0080). Two refs resolving to the same manifest digest is a fact about the registry rather than about the image's contents, and it is the only thing that answers the clause's question. It also performs the user's action rather than modelling it: a bare `docker pull` is what the README quick start tells a self-hoster to run, and each ref is measured by its OWN pull, because inspecting a shared local image would carry both RepoDigests once the second pull is a cache hit -- a check that cannot fail.\n\nWHAT IS STILL UNPROVEN, stated because the clause is discharged and the job is not: it has never executed. Every guard that makes it safe also makes it untestable before a non-pre-release tag, and the rc will skip it by design. The awk digest extraction WAS tested locally against a real `docker pull` transcript, against empty input (the empty-guard fires) and against a two-Digest input (fails loudly rather than passing silently), so the parsing is proven and the job is not.\n\nCLAUSE (4) IS ANSWERED, 2026-09-21, by item/about-version-defined (issue #289), and the answer is: THE LOOP SETS IT, in the ledger pass that follows the v1.0.0 tag's release.yml run concluding success. Restated here rather than merely pointed at, because a clause that cites another item for its content is a clause the next reader has to assemble.\n\nWHAT about.version MEANS, now that it has a definition: the ledger's record of the latest git tag pushed to this repository, written without the leading `v`. It is NOT config('doccum.version') (the version the tree would build, '0.1.0-dev' outside a release build) and NOT a prediction of the next tag. docs/ledger/vocab.md's `version on the about node` section holds the definition and the reasoning; this clause holds only what this item has to DO with it.\n\nSO CLAUSE (4) NOW REQUIRES, mechanically: after the v1.0.0 tag's release.yml run has concluded success, the next ledger pass sets about.version to the string \"1.0.0\", and no earlier. Earlier is the failure mode that matters, because this item's own first clause READS this field and LOOP.md's stopping condition is this item reaching Completed -- LOOP.md does not name about.version itself, so the dependency runs through this doneWhen -- and a value written ahead of the release would let the loop stop on its own prediction. The field is defined to lag and never to lead, and this is the practice that keeps that true.\n\nTHE PREMISE IN 'CLAUSES (1) AND (4) ARE UNCHANGED' ABOVE IS WITHDRAWN, not the paragraph. It says clause (4) 'is issue #289, which is the owner's ... decision/0105 puts schema changes with the owner, so the loop states the choice rather than making it'. decision/0117 retracted that premise on 2026-09-21: the phrase had no source before the decision that coined it, and three schema changes to context.jsonld on 2026-09-18 were all made by loop items. The paragraph stays as written because the ledger is append-only for history and because what it observed about CHANGELOG.md is still true; what changed is who was entitled to answer, and the answer is above. about.version was 0.1.0 when that paragraph was written and is null now, since no tag has ever been pushed.\n\nCLAUSE (3) IS NOT AT RISK, AND THE REPOSITORY'S OWN ACCOUNT OF WHY WAS WRONG, 2026-09-21, issue #348. The clause requires `docker pull ghcr.io/turbophp/doccum` with no tag to resolve to 1.0.0, so it depends entirely on a v1.0.0 tag push publishing `latest`. release.yml's comment at lines 105-118 states that before the raw entry at line 131 was added, \"a v1.0.0 tag would therefore have published 1.0.0 and 1.0 and no `latest` at all, leaving clause (3) unsatisfiable and the quick starts pulling a tag that does not exist\". THAT IS FALSE, and it is false in the reassuring direction: the clause was never unsatisfiable.\n\nWHERE `latest` ACTUALLY COMES FROM, checked at the SHA release.yml pins (docker/metadata-action@dc80280, v6.2.0) rather than recalled. `grep -rn flavor .github/` returns nothing, so the `flavor` input's `latest` option sits at its documented default of `auto`, and the README's \"Latest tag\" section says verbatim that under auto it \"will be generated by default\" for `type=semver,pattern=...` among others. the `image` job's `tags:` list DOES carry `type=semver,pattern={{version}}`. Confirmed in the action's source at that SHA, not from the README alone: src/meta.ts's procSemver sets `latest = true` on the non-prerelease branch and leaves it false on the prerelease branch, and generateTags appends `:latest` whenever version.latest is set. So a non-pre-release tag push has always published `latest`, through a mechanism the `tags:` list does not mention and reading the `tags:` list cannot reveal.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR THIS CLAUSE: `latest` on a stable tag comes from flavor latest=auto via the type=semver entry, NOT from a raw entry. the `type=raw,value=latest,enable=${{ ... }}` entry enables `latest` on exactly the condition auto already enables it, so it is redundant -- harmless, since setVersion only assigns version.latest while it is undefined and the first type=semver entry therefore decides the flag, but it does add a second literal `latest` to steps.meta.outputs.tags because generateTags does not deduplicate. Anyone editing that `tags:` list to satisfy this clause should know that DELETING THE SEMVER ENTRY, not the raw one, is what would break the pull-with-no-tag guarantee. See decision/0122.\n\nCLAUSE (1)'S \"BEFORE THE TAG IS PUSHED\" HALF IS ENFORCED RATHER THAN ASKED, 2026-09-21, in PR #359 (issue #358). The clause required \"a [1.0.0] section exists and the changelog-section job passes for it BEFORE the tag is pushed\" -- an instruction to a person, with nothing checking it. Verified by parsing release.yml rather than reading it: `image` needed only `verify`, and changelog-section.sh for the real tag first ran inside `release`, which needs [image, boot-published-image]. So a tag with a missing or typo'd heading pushed the image AND `latest` to ghcr and only then failed, which is the half-published state item/changelog-release-notes exists to prevent, produced by its own guard.\n\nA `changelog-section` job now runs the extractor against GITHUB_REF_NAME and `image` needs it, so the failure happens before anything reaches the registry. It is NOT `if:`-gated on a tag: release.yml also fires on workflow_dispatch, a skipped job is not a successful one for `needs:`, and `image` depends on this one -- so rather than rely on how Actions propagates a skip, the job always runs and exits 0 when there is no tag, keeping the dispatch path mutation/0030 established.\n\nWHAT REMAINS OF CLAUSE (1) IS THE SECTION ITSELF. CHANGELOG.md still has no [1.0.0] heading, and writing one now would presume the answer to issue #302 -- under option (C) the existing [0.1.0] section is what becomes [1.0.0]. The gate does not create the section; it refuses to publish without it.\n\nassert-release-gate.py's single REQUIRED_DEPENDENCY became a LIST of required needs-edges to carry this, and mutation/0057 exists specifically to prove that widening it did not weaken the edge decision/0120 put there.\n\nTHE SENTENCE ABOVE IS OUT OF DATE, 2026-09-21, and is corrected here rather than deleted so the reasoning that produced it stays legible (decision/0129). verify-latest-resolves-to-the-tag is NO LONGER guarded `startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/') && !contains(github.ref_name, '-')` and is no longer narrower than its siblings. PR #366 gave it exactly the gate `release` carries and moved the pre-release exclusion inside the job, because `release` now needs it and a skipped `needs` dependency propagates its skip -- left as it was, an rc tag would have produced no GitHub Release at all. CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM'S CLAUSE (3): on an rc the job now RUNS and SUCCEEDS having asserted nothing, printing that it did so. So a green check named \"a bare docker pull resolves to this tag\" is not evidence for clause (3) on a pre-release tag; only a non-pre-release tag exercises the digest comparison, and v1.0.0 is still the first tag on which it will ever execute.\n\nEVERY `release.yml:NNN` CITATION IN THIS FIELD IS REPOINTED, 2026-09-21, to an anchor that moves with the thing it names (issue #371, decision/0129). The line numbers were all wrong -- not by one, but by seventy-odd, because jobs were inserted above them by pull requests that never opened this file. The claims themselves were re-verified by locating each anchor by CONTENT before the swap, so what changed is the address and not the assertion.",
      "actionStatus": "PotentialActionStatus",
