    {
      "@id": "item/search-indexer-null-subject",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 70,
      "name": "Indexing a property whose subject is soft-deleted throws a TypeError",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/242",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:8-search"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "SearchIndexer::forProperty() reads $property->subject and passes it to directoryFor(Model $subject). subject is a morphTo, so the related model's SoftDeletes scope applies and it resolves to NULL once the subject is trashed -- into a parameter that is not nullable. The return type is already ?Directory and both consumers handle null ($directory?->getKey(), $directory?->ancestorIds() ?? []), so the nullable case was anticipated one line further down and only the parameter type disagrees. Reached through SearchProjectionObserver::saved(), which indexes on every Property save, so any write to a property on a trashed file or directory hits it. FOUND BY ACCIDENT, which is why it is filed rather than fixed inside item/directory-force-delete-orphans: a test there built properties on already-trashed subjects and three tests died here before reaching an assertion. That branch reordered its fixture instead of patching this, so the defect stayed visible. Done when directoryFor() accepts ?Model (or forProperty() handles the null before calling it), with a test that indexes a property on a soft-deleted subject and fails with the parameter type as it stands today. Fix this WITH item/directory-policy-trashed-update, not instead of it: a nullable parameter alone converts a crash into a silently permitted edit, which is worse.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T21:46:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T22:02:38Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/254"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/directory-policy-trashed-update",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 71,
      "name": "DirectoryPolicy::update() has no trashed guard, where FilePolicy::update() does",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/247",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "FilePolicy::update() refuses a trashed file, with its reasoning stated in place: editing something delete() already hid is a reveal update() must not reopen. DirectoryPolicy::update() has no equivalent -- it returns access->can($user, $directory, Edit) and nothing else. The second authorisation layer does not cover for it, DELIBERATELY: DirectoryAccess::resolve() checks PROPER ancestors only, excluding the directory's own trashed state, because restoring a directory is a manage check that can only ever be reached while it is trashed (issue #49). So a directory trashed on its own still resolves Edit from a grant above it, layer two passes, layer one has no guard, and PropertyPanel::save()'s authorize('update', $subject) lets a metadata edit through. Both layers must pass and here only one is asking. NOT VERIFIED and not claimed: whether today's UI offers a route to a trashed directory's property panel. That does not decide it -- in a two-layer model the policy is the guard of record, and 'no current screen happens to reach it' is the state in which a guard silently stops existing -- but practical severity today may be low. Done when DirectoryPolicy::update() refuses a trashed directory, MUTATION-CHECKED per CLAUDE.md: delete the guard, watch the test fail, restore it. Check DirectoryPolicy's other verbs against FilePolicy's in the same pass; this was found by reading two files side by side, not by a sweep, so it should not be assumed to be the only gap.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T21:46:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T22:02:38Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/254"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/directory-policy-trashed-move",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 72,
      "name": "DirectoryPolicy::move() has no trashed guard either, and moving INTO the trash corrupts path",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/253",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "Created and Completed in one pass, which is unusual and is explained rather than hidden: this is the THIRD instance of one defect class already carrying two ledger items (70 and 71), it was found while fixing the second, and the previous iteration had wrongly reported the reachable v1 queue as empty. Filing it as Potential and completing it an hour later would have recorded a plan that never existed.\n\nmove() had no trashed() check on $directory. DirectoryAccess::resolve() checks proper ancestors only, deliberately excluding a directory's own trashed state so restore stays reachable (issue #49), so a directory trashed on its own still resolved Manage from a grant above it -- item 71's gap on a different verb, and worse to leave open, because MoveDirectory rewrites path and recomputes depth across the subtree and hasTrashedProperAncestor() is computed from those columns.\n\nA SECOND defect, of a different kind, surfaced while deciding whether a trashed $newParent should also refuse. Not a reveal but CORRUPTION: Directory::syncPath() resolves the parent through self::query() -- the default scope, which on a SoftDeletes model excludes trashed rows -- so a trashed $newParent makes that lookup return null, (string) null is '', and the moved subtree's path rebuilds as \"5/\" instead of \"/3/5/\", missing the parent's segment and the leading slash, in exactly the column ancestorIds() and hasTrashedProperAncestor() both read for every later access decision. Verified at Directory.php:113-117 with SoftDeletes at line 42, not inferred.\n\nDone when move() refuses both, each mutation-checked through .github/mutations.json, which the guards job executes. Unreachable through today's UI -- all four authorize('move', ...) sites in Browser.php resolve their destination via Directory::query()->findOrFail(), excluding trashed rows -- and guarded anyway on item 71's reasoning: in a two-layer model the policy is the guard of record, and 'no current screen reaches it' is the state in which a guard quietly stops existing.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T23:45:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T23:59:51Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/256"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/palette-smoke",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 73,
      "name": "The command palette is driven by the container smoke",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/276",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "container-smoke.mjs opens the palette with Ctrl-K from a page that is NOT /search, searches in it, and opens a hit -- and at least one of those assertions has been SHOWN to fail against a deliberately broken image, with the run recorded as a Mutation here.\n\nWHY THIS ITEM EXISTS AT ALL, stated plainly rather than left to be inferred from its order. `grep -n palette .github/scripts/container-smoke.mjs` returned nothing: the surface people reach search through most often had never been executed in a browser by anything in this repository. tests/Feature/Search/PaletteTest.php covers the component thoroughly and every clause it can reach is server side -- openPalette() sets a flag, hits() honours the viewer's reach, destinationFor() resolves a route. Everything that makes the palette a palette rather than a Livewire property is browser side: the window Ctrl-K binding, the open-search-palette CustomEvent and its listener, wire:model.live on an input the opening round trip creates, Alpine's selected/openSelected navigation, and the swap of the index's private-use markers into <mark>. CLAUDE.md says a Blade assertion renders the markup for all of that and cannot see that none of it booted.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM IS NOT. It is TESTING DEBT under spec:14-testing, and its clause is bounded to \"the palette is driven by the smoke\". It is deliberately NOT an item that brings \"the palette surface\" under audit, and filing it does not answer the accounting question decision/0102 puts to the owner. It is one of the three options offered there, taken for one surface only -- and partly forced: LedgerValidator requires Mutation.implements and PullRequest.implements to resolve to an Action, so mutation/0032 and the PR nodes for #277 and #280 could not exist in this file without an item to name. Recording that here rather than presenting the item as a neutral bookkeeping choice.\n\nWHAT IT LEAVES OPEN, so that Completed is not read as coverage. One of the check's four assertions is mutation-proven; see mutation/0032. The escape-before-swap ORDER is unproven and is issue #279. move() is not driven at all. /search's own results page still carries no data-test hooks, so snippet rendering there remains browser-unproven.\nBORN COMPLETED, and saying so on item/directory-policy-trashed-move's model rather than leaving a reader of the ledger to discover it from git. This item first entered ledger.jsonld at 59dae8f already CompletedActionStatus: it was filed and closed inside the hour it was built, and the doneWhen above was written knowing what had been built. What stood in for a prior clause is issue #276, opened before the PR and holding the requirement in its own words; that is better than nothing and it is not the same as a plan the ledger recorded in advance. See decision/0105, and decision/0068 on why a clause written afterwards is indistinguishable from one that was met.\n\nIt also did not move the gap. v1 went 66 of 72 to 67 of 73 when this item landed; six items remained before and after. An item that enters Completed cannot change the number remaining.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-19T20:30:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-19T21:19:00Z",
