    {
      "@id": "item/snippet-escape-order",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 74,
      "name": "The search snippet is escaped before its markers become <mark>",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/279",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "A document whose TEXT contains markup next to the searched word is uploaded, searched, and the snippet asserted to carry that markup as literal characters -- not as an element -- on both the palette hit and the /search results row; and the guard is mutation-checked, per CLAUDE.md.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. Both surfaces render document text through {!! !!}. Fts5SearchIndex deliberately wraps matched terms in two PRIVATE-USE codepoints rather than in HTML, so the view can escape the whole passage first and only then swap those two for real <mark> tags. Escape-then-swap is safe; swap-then-escape renders the tags as text; never escaping renders the document's own bytes as markup -- and the passage comes from an uploaded file, so an uploader chooses them. Both views say exactly this in their own comments.\n\nNOTHING PROVED IT. SearchPageTest's fixture body was \"the tenant shall maintain the premises in good repair\" -- no '<', '&' or quote anywhere -- so the e() call could be deleted from either view and every test stayed green. The existing snippet test asserts the <mark> element, which survives that deletion untouched. A guard whose test passes without it is the state CLAUDE.md calls worse than none, because it looks protected.\n\nHOW IT IS PROVEN NOW. One test per surface, each with its own document bodied 'the <b>tenant</b> shall & \"maintain\" the premises', and three assertions that PARTITION the failure modes rather than pooling them: assertSee('&lt;b&gt;') catches \"never escapes\", assertDontSee('<b>') catches the same mode from the other side, and assertSee('<mark ...>tenant</mark>') catches \"swap-then-escape\" -- which escapes the document's markup correctly and breaks only the highlight, so neither of the first two can see it. Both fixtures are created INSIDE their own test rather than in beforeEach, so no sibling assertion changes.\n\nThe mutation is TWO .github/mutations.json entries rather than a hand-run branch, so `guards` asserts both directions on every push and the guard cannot quietly rot. See mutation/0036 and mutation/0037.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM IS NOT, on the same footing as item/palette-smoke. Testing debt under spec:14-testing, bounded to this one ordering property. It is not a claim about what the ledger tracks and does not answer decision/0102's question; it exists as an item partly because LedgerValidator requires a Mutation's and a PullRequest's implements to resolve to an Action.\n\nWHAT REMAINS UNPROVEN, recorded so Completed is not read as coverage. resources/views/livewire/search/results.blade.php still carries no data-test hooks, and the container smoke still does not drive /search's snippet in a BROWSER -- these are Livewire component tests asserting rendered HTML, which is a different thing. The issue's original clause guessed that a data-test hook would be needed first; it was not, and that guess is recorded here rather than silently dropped.\nBORN COMPLETED, same as item/palette-smoke and for the same reasons, recorded here rather than left to git. This item first entered ledger.jsonld at 23f3d41 already CompletedActionStatus. Issue #279 held the prior statement -- and, as the clause above already notes, got one thing wrong about what the work would need, which is exactly why an issue is not a substitute for a ledger clause. See decision/0105.\n\nIt did not move the gap either: 67 of 73 to 68 of 74, six remaining on both sides.\n\nONE MORE BOUND WORTH STATING, since \"bounded to this one ordering property\" invites the question of how large the class is. `grep -rn '{!!' resources/views` returns THREE sites: the two snippet renderers this item covers, and resources/views/livewire/settings/security.blade.php line 167, which renders a QR code SVG produced by the application itself rather than text from an uploaded file. That third site is a different concern -- not an ordering, and not attacker-chosen input -- so it is outside this item on purpose. Naming it, and the count, is what lets a reader see the class is closed at three rather than assume it.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-19T22:45:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-19T23:06:10Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/284"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/reach-existence-oracle",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 75,
      "name": "A subject outside the viewer's reach answers the same as one that does not exist",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/109",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "size": "M",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "THE QUESTION ISSUE #109 ASKS IS NOT '404 OR 403'. That framing survived four days and is wrong, and the restatement is the point of this item (decision/0068: a clause written after the fact is indistinguishable from having met the original one, so the clause is written BEFORE any code moves). The security property at stake is whether a surface gives the caller an EXISTENCE ORACLE: a surface leaks iff a subject that exists but lies outside the viewer's reach answers DIFFERENTLY from an id that does not exist at all. Whether the shared answer is 404 or 403 is a local choice; that the two agree is the requirement.\n\nWHY THE OLD FRAMING WAS WRONG, established from the code rather than argued. The case for 404 was always anti-enumeration. FileVersionDownloadController achieves anti-enumeration with 403 and says so in its own test: 'A version id that genuinely exists (on a file the caller cannot view) and one that does not exist at all must answer identically: 403 either way ... there is no existence oracle here.' Its sibling test asserts 404 for a cross-file version id when the caller can view both files. That controller is internally coherent and it picks the opposite status from the movers. So the product does not have two postures in conflict; it has some surfaces that close the oracle (either way) and some that leave it open.\n\nWHERE IT IS OPEN, and why. A bare `findOrFail($id)` followed by `authorize()` answers 404 for a nonexistent id (ModelNotFoundException) and 403 for one that exists outside the viewer's reach. Those differ, so the pair is an oracle. A lookup scoped to reach INSIDE the query answers 404 for both. Route-model binding that refuses the parent before the child is looked up answers 403 for both.\n\nCLOSED TODAY (no work): moveFile() 641-647, moveDirectory() 706-714, trashDirectoryRow() 449-453, the 18 /api/v1 routes via Controller.php:56,70, TrashRestoreController 50,67 -- all scoped, both cases 404. FileVersionDownloadController -- both cases 403.\n\nOPEN TODAY, all in app/Livewire/Files/Browser.php, and this is the work: dropMove() destination 587, dropMove() file subject 590, dropMove() directory subject 604, trashFileRow() 431, preview() 476, selectDirectory() 270-291.\n\nCLAUSE 1 -- dropMove()'s DESTINATION, which needs no posture decision and may land alone and first. item/api-content settled that a move destination outside the viewer's reach answers 404, and swept the two form-based movers. Drag-and-drop is the THIRD mover and kept the old shape; the 2026-09-20 comment on issue #109 pointed at line 587 and it was read as a correction to a stale patch rather than as a missed call site. Scope the destination lookup to DirectoryAccess::viewableDirectoryIds() inside the query with abort_if(null, 404), exactly as moveFile() does. FileBrowserDragTest:72 ('refuses a drop into a directory the viewer cannot reach') flips from assertForbidden to assertNotFound. Done when that test asserts 404 and a sibling asserts a NONEXISTENT destination id answers 404 too -- the pair, not the single case, is what proves the oracle closed.\n\nCLAUSE 2 -- THE REMAINING FIVE SITES EACH CLOSE THE ORACLE, by whichever route suits the site, and each carries a TEST PAIR asserting the unreachable-existing and the nonexistent case answer the same. Scoping is the simpler route for the two dropMove() subjects and trashFileRow(); selectDirectory() is the one that needs thought, because its existing comment argues against scoping on the grounds that it would swap a policy decision for an existence check -- an argument about WHICH status, which this clause does not require it to concede. It may close the oracle at 403 instead, the way FileVersionDownloadController does. What it may not do is keep answering 403 and 404 for the two cases.\n\nCLAUSE 3 -- preview()'s DOCBLOCK IS FALSE and is corrected whether or not its code changes. It claims 'The id is re-resolved against the viewer's reach rather than trusted, because it arrives from the client like any other'; line 476 is File::query()->findOrFail($fileId) with no reach scoping, and authorize('view') is what refuses. The behaviour is safe; the sentence is not, and CLAUDE.md treats a claim in a comment like any other claim.\n\nCLAUSE 4 -- MUTATION-CHECKED, because these are security tests and CLAUDE.md is explicit: delete the guard, confirm the test fails, restore it. A test pair that passes with the scoping removed proves nothing, and the failure mode is specific here -- if only the unreachable-existing half is asserted, removing the scoping flips it to 403 and the test still fails for the wrong reason. The nonexistent half is what makes the pair load-bearing.\n\nTHE NINE TESTS THAT HOLD THE CURRENT ANSWERS, so no later reader has to re-derive the set: FileBrowserActionsTest:84, FileBrowserTest:91, FileBrowserDragTest:72, FileBrowserDragTest:84, FileBrowserRowActionsTest:51, FileBrowserRowActionsTest:196, FilePreviewTest:132, PropertyPanelTest:39, FileVersionDownloadTest:120,124. Counted by reading, not grepping: non-API assertForbidden() appears about forty times across eighteen files and the rest concern permission LEVEL (without edit access, manage alone is not enough, admin pages), not reach. DirectoryArchiveTest:173 and FileDownloadTest:51 look close and are not candidates -- the first is requester identity, the second an absent grant.\n\nTHIS ITEM RAISES THE v1 GAP FROM SIX TO SEVEN, and that is said plainly per decision/0105 rather than left for a reader to notice. It is v1 because it is an information disclosure, however small, in a product whose entire premise is per-directory access control, and because item/v1-audit would have to raise it anyway. It exists at all because decision/0110 retracted the invented premise that creating an item is the owner's.\n\nCLAUSE 1 IS DISCHARGED, 2026-09-21, in PR #305. dropMove()'s destination lookup is scoped to DirectoryAccess::viewableDirectoryIds() inside the query with abort_if($destination === null, 404), the same shape moveFile() uses. Two tests, because one case cannot show that two answers match: 'refuses a drop into a directory the viewer cannot reach, and moves nothing' flipped from assertForbidden to assertNotFound, and 'answers a destination that does not exist the same way as one out of reach' is new and uses max(id)+1 rather than a literal so it cannot start passing for the wrong reason. Registered as mutation/0038 and proven in the guards job both directions.\n\nTHE ITEM STAYS ACTIVE. Clause 2's five sites are untouched -- dropMove()'s two SUBJECTS (590, 604), trashFileRow() (431), preview() (476) and selectDirectory() (270-291) -- and clause 3's preview() docblock is still false. Marking the item Completed on one clause would be decision/0087's substitution, counting the rest as done because the first part is.\n\nAND CLAUSE 1 COST AN EXISTING GUARD ITS WITNESS, which is recorded here because it is a fact about this item's work and not only about the mutation harness. Scoping the destination moved the refusal earlier in dropMove(), so a-dropped-row-is-re-authorised-at-both-ends -- which removes that method's authorize('move', ...) -- stopped being provable by the out-of-reach test it had always named. The guards job caught it on the first push. See decision/0111.\n\nCLAUSES 2 AND 3 DISCHARGED, 2026-09-21, in PRs #307 and #308. Every site this item enumerates is closed: dropMove()'s destination (clause 1, #305), its file and directory subjects, trashFileRow() and preview() (#307), and selectDirectory()'s root branch (#308). Each carries a TEST PAIR and a registered mutation proven both directions; the guards job reported 99 then 100 load-bearing, read entry by entry from the log rather than by the job's colour.\n\nTWO SITES TURNED OUT NOT TO NEED WORK, which is worth recording because both were assumed to. selectDirectory()'s NESTED branch was already closed -- it scopes to children of the directory being browsed, and access inherits down the subtree, so a child of a visible directory is visible and a non-child answers 404 whether it exists or not. And PropertyPanel is not a site at all: mount() receives an already-resolved model, so there is no lookup to scope.\n\nCLAUSE 3 WAS DISCHARGED BY CODE RATHER THAN BY PROSE. preview()'s docblock claimed the id was \"re-resolved against the viewer's reach rather than trusted\" while the lookup did no scoping at all. Clause 2 made the sentence true instead of deleting it.\n\nTHIS ITEM'S BOUNDARY IS NOW STATED, because its enumeration was incomplete and the correction belongs here rather than only on the issue. It covers app/Livewire/Files/Browser.php and nothing else. The ROUTE-MODEL-BINDING surfaces -- /files/{directory?}, FilePreviewController, FileDownloadController and FileVersionDownloadController's {file} -- carry the same open oracle and are NOT in scope here; they are item/reach-oracle-route-binding. See decision/0112 for why they were split rather than folded in.\n\nCOMPLETED 2026-09-21. Every site this item enumerates answers identically for a subject that exists outside the viewer's reach and for an id that does not exist, each proven by a TEST PAIR and a registered mutation the guards job re-runs on every push (mutation/0038 and 0039-0043). The item's boundary is app/Livewire/Files/Browser.php; the route-model-binding surfaces are item/reach-oracle-route-binding and were never in scope here, which decision/0112 records along with the four ways this item's own enumeration was wrong.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T08:55:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T10:00:00Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/305",
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/307",
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/308"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/reach-oracle-route-binding",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 76,
      "name": "Route-model-bound subjects answer the same whether unreachable or absent",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/109",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "size": "M",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "Route-model binding resolves a subject BEFORE any policy runs, so the router answers \"does this id exist\" and the policy answers \"may you reach it\" -- and they answer differently by construction. A nonexistent id 404s from the binding; one that exists outside the viewer's reach is bound and then 403s from authorize(). That difference is an existence oracle, the same property item/reach-existence-oracle closed across Browser.php, on a surface that item does not cover.\n\nTHE SITES. This enumeration said FOUR and was wrong within a day, which is decision/0112 firing a second time -- on the item that decision created. It is FIVE. The four originally named: /files/{directory?} -> Browser::mount() (routes/web.php); FilePreviewController::__invoke(File $file); FileDownloadController::__invoke(File $file); FileVersionDownloadController::__invoke(File $file, int $version), on its {file} parameter only.\n\nAND THE FIFTH, missed: DirectoryArchiveDownloadController::__invoke(DirectoryArchive $archive), on /directories/archives/{archive}/download. A nonexistent archive id 404s from the binding; one that exists but belongs to someone else is bound and then answers 403 from `abort_unless($archive->requested_by === auth()->id(), 403)`. Same shape, same leak -- whether a given archive id exists, across every user's archive jobs.\n\nHOW IT WAS FOUND, because the method is the point. The original census walked Browser.php and the /api/v1 controllers. This one came from asking a different question -- not \"which lookups look unscoped\" but \"which route parameters are resolved by IMPLICIT MODEL BINDING at all\" -- and answering it by grepping every controller signature and Livewire mount() for a Model type-hint. That enumeration is checkable: implicit binding happens exactly when a route parameter's type-hint is a Model, so the set of signatures IS the set of sites. The earlier one was a list of places someone had looked.\n\nTHE ORACLE HERE IS CONFIRMED FROM THE TEST, not reasoned about: DirectoryArchiveTest's \"hands a built archive only to the person who asked for it\" asserts assertForbidden() for another user's archive, and the model carries no global scope (its booted() only assigns a uuid), so a nonexistent id takes the binding's 404. The two disagree.\n\nAND FIXING IT WILL DISARM A REGISTERED GUARD, which is decision/0111 and must be handled as that decision says rather than by weakening the entry. mutations.json's `a-directory-archive-reaches-only-its-requester` removes exactly that abort_unless line, and its witness is the assertForbidden() test above. Scoping the lookup to requested_by moves the refusal earlier, so removing the abort_unless would stop changing that test's outcome. Unlike decision/0111's case there is no other test that still reaches it -- scoping makes the line unreachable, not merely unwitnessed -- so the entry is to be REPLACED by one on the scoping itself, with its `why` recording which entry it succeeds and why the old guard stopped being reachable. Nothing about the protection weakens; the guard and its proof move together.\n\nTHE API IS NOT A SITE, and this was checked rather than assumed, because it is the obvious next worry. Every /api/v1 route declares `int $file` / `int $directory` and resolves through Api\\V1\\Controller::viewableFileOrFail() / viewableDirectoryOrFail(), which scope by the caller's reach inside the query. Implicit binding never runs there, so the API has neither the oracle nor any exposure to a fix applied here. This also rules out one candidate fix outright: Route::bind() is keyed on parameter NAME, not type, so a global binder for 'file' would substitute a File model into every `int $file` API signature and TypeError the whole API.\n\nONE OF THOSE NEEDS ITS RECORD CORRECTED, and the correction is part of this item because the mistaken claim is what hid the surface. FileVersionDownloadController was described as \"closing the oracle at 403\", and that is true of its VERSION id: with an unviewable file, an existing and a nonexistent version both answer 403, because the refusal happens before the version lookup is reached, and its own test says so. It is FALSE of the FILE id, where the binding/policy split is wide open. The controller closes one oracle and leaves another.\n\nTHE FIX SHAPE IS DIFFERENT FROM THE BROWSER.PHP ONE, which is the substantive reason this is its own item. In-query scoping cannot reach a parameter the router resolved before the controller was entered. The options are a scoped binding (Route::bind or a scoped implicit binding that filters by the viewer's reach) or moving the lookup into the controller body and scoping it there. Which of those, and whether the shared answer is 404 or 403, is for whoever builds it -- the requirement is only that the two cases AGREE, per the property above.\n\nDONE WHEN each of the four sites answers identically for a subject that exists outside the viewer's reach and for an id that does not exist; each carries a TEST PAIR asserting both halves, because one case cannot show that two answers match; and each is registered in .github/mutations.json with an expectFailing that has been shown to flip -- naming which half of the pair the entry actually proves, since removing a scope typically breaks only the out-of-reach half (decision/0111). FileVersionDownloadController's existing VERSION-id coverage stays as it is and is not the witness for its FILE-id guard.\n\nTHIS RAISES THE v1 GAP FROM SEVEN TO EIGHT, said plainly per decision/0105 rather than left for a reader to notice. It is v1 on the same grounds as its sibling: an information disclosure, however small, in a product whose entire premise is per-directory access control, and one item/v1-audit would have to raise anyway. Filed by the loop under decision/0110, which retracted the invented premise that creating an item is the owner's.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T09:55:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T11:00:00Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/315",
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/316"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/root-docs",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 77,
      "name": "CONTRIBUTING.md and SECURITY.md, the two root files section 12 names",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/322",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "Spec section 12 names four root files -- README.md, LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING.md and SECURITY.md -- and milestone 11 names them again. Two of the four were absent from the tree, and NO backlog item covered either, so nothing in this loop was going to produce them; item/v1-audit found them, and issues #321 and #322 record them. One item rather than two because section 12 names them in one sentence and the first must reference the second. Done when CONTRIBUTING.md exists at the repository root and condenses CLAUDE.md for a human audience -- Node 22 and why, the conventions, the six seams, both authorisation layers, the upgrade-path rules, the testing rules that are not guessable, and the exact set of CI jobs a pull request will get; and when SECURITY.md exists where GitHub reads it, naming a private reporting channel, supported versions, expected response times stated honestly for a small project, and a scope section written from doccum's own threat model -- bypass of either authorisation layer independently, reach leaks through response differences, presigned-URL problems, the first-run installer on a populated instance, and a token reaching purge -- rather than from a template. Every operator-facing claim in SECURITY.md is checked against the CODE, not against the spec, because the file is a promise to a reader who cannot check it.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T13:15:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T13:17:48Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/325"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/openapi-spec-reconcile",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 78,
      "name": "Section 12 specifies Scramble and /docs/api; neither exists and no decision rules on it",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/323",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "Spec section 12 says the integrator reference is \"generated by dedoc/scramble from route signatures, FormRequests, and API Resources\" and \"served interactively at /docs/api outside production\". Neither is true: the package is absent from composer.json, the builder is hand-rolled at app/Support/OpenApi/OpenApiSpecBuilder.php, and no route serves /docs/api. item/openapi-reference is Completed and left no trace of why its named dependency went unused. The substitution is probably RIGHT -- section 12's stated reason for Scramble is that the reference cannot drift from the code, and tests/Feature/Api/OpenApiContractTest.php delivers that property more directly, failing the build on drift in either direction rather than regenerating silently. The defect is the silence, which is the same defect item/spec-reconcile and item/spec-section8-reconcile exist to fix elsewhere. Done when a decision node records the substitution and its reasoning, section 12 and milestone 11 read as what is true, and the /docs/api question is disposed of EXPLICITLY -- either the route exists, or the amendment says the committed openapi.json is the whole deliverable. Both are acceptable; leaving it unanswered is not.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T13:32:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T13:44:05Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/327"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/validator-skip-guard-tested",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 79,
      "name": "The validator's silent-skip guard has no test and no mutation entry",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/311",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "RESTATED, because the first version of this clause asserted something false and it reached main in PR #326. It said the CLI wrapper .github/scripts/validate-ledger.php \"has NO test at all\", so both guards added for issue #311 were unwitnessed. That is wrong. ledger.yml carries a job named \"validate-ledger.php refuses a silent skip\" with FOUR steps: the env-var refusal, the not-run notice, a positive control asserting the positional form still prints its Compared line, and a check that --witnesses is unaffected by MAIN_SHA in the environment. I wrote the false premise after reading tests/ and not .github/workflows/ -- decision/0114 exactly, checking the places I thought of rather than asking the question the code answers, and the third instance of it in one session. WHAT WAS ACTUALLY MISSING was not a test but EVIDENCE that the test works. The job's own comment claimed \"delete the guard and this step exits 0 instead\" -- an assertion about a mutation nobody had run, which CLAUDE.md forbids citing. Done when both guards have been mutated and the result recorded: mutation/0049 for the env-var refusal (died at step 1) and mutation/0050 for the not-run notice (died at step 2), two mutants failing two different steps so neither stands in for the other. AND the final clause of issue #311 -- \"registered in .github/mutations.json IF the guard is one the mutation sweep can witness\" -- is answered NO, on a structural fact: mutation-check.php runs `php artisan test --filter=<pattern>` and classifies the JUnit log, so expectFailing must name a PEST test. A guard whose test is a bash step in a workflow can never be matched by --filter; registering it would produce the \"matched no test at all\" manifest error rather than a witness. So this guard pair is witnessed by CI and mutated by hand, and that is the mechanism's shape rather than a gap left open.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T13:45:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T14:05:05Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/330"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/about-version-defined",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 80,
      "name": "about.version has no definition, and tag-v1-0-0's fourth clause cannot be answered",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/289",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/vocab-resolves"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "RESTATED BEFORE THE WORK, per decision/0068, because attempting the first clause found it unsatisfiable as written. It said \"context.jsonld defines about.version in one sentence that distinguishes it from the other two readings\". It cannot. docs/ledger/context.jsonld is STRICT JSON -- no comment syntax -- and a JSON-LD term definition is an object whose keys are keywords (@id, @type, @container and the rest), not a place prose can live. The attempt is what established this: adding the sentence as a // comment made the file fail json.load on line 15. SO THE DEFINITION NEEDS A HOME, and the home is the vocabulary document item/vocab-resolves builds -- which is the same gap seen from the other side, since #294 is precisely that @vocab points at a docs/ledger/vocab path that does not exist and every project term therefore resolves to nothing. This item now dependsOn that one rather than duplicating it. THE READING IS STILL THIS ITEM'S TO CHOOSE, and the choice is narrowed by a fact found while restating: doccum already has exactly one canonical notion of its own version, config('doccum.version'), which reads DOCCUM_VERSION and falls back to '0.1.0-dev'; release.yml sets it from the tag. So \"the version the working tree would build\" is already named elsewhere and about.version must not become a second reader of it (decision/0080). AND THE CURRENT VALUE IS FALSE UNDER EVERY READING BUT ONE. about.version is '0.1.0' and `git tag` lists NOTHING -- no release, no image, no Release. It cannot name a released version, and it does not match config/doccum.php's '0.1.0-dev' either. Done when: the vocabulary document defines about.version in one sentence that distinguishes released-version from would-build-version from next-tag-prediction; the current value is corrected to match the chosen reading, with a note saying it was wrong and why; and item/tag-v1-0-0's clause (4) -- \"say who updates the ledger's release node after the tag\" -- is restated to name who sets it and when, which decision/0117 has already established is the loop rather than the owner.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T15:00:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T15:12:02Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/334"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/vocab-resolves",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 81,
      "name": "The ledger's @vocab points at a path that does not exist",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/294",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "context.jsonld's @vocab is https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/tree/main/docs/ledger/vocab# and there is no docs/ledger/vocab in this repository. Terms mapped to schema: resolve; everything project-specific falls through to @vocab and so resolves to nothing -- doneWhen, touched, outcome, size, release, order, mergeSha, testsConclusion, ledgerConclusion, expectFailing, implements, mergedIn, affects, supersedes. Those are the terms carrying this project's actual meaning, and their definitions live in LedgerValidator's enums, LOOP.md's prose, and the accumulated argument inside individual doneWhen strings. A @vocab IRI is a CITATION -- it says the definitions are over there -- and nothing has ever checked it, which is CLAUDE.md's own standard applied to the context file. Unblocked by decision/0117 for the same reason as item/about-version-defined. Done when either docs/ledger/vocab.md exists with one sentence per term and @vocab points at it, OR @vocab is dropped and each project term is mapped explicitly with a note that its definition lives in LedgerValidator; and either way LedgerValidator gains a rule that every non-schema term resolves to something that exists, with a witness entry that trips it, since the current state would fail that rule and that is the point. Explicitly NOT acceptable: repointing @vocab at something that merely returns 200 without writing the definitions, which converts a visible gap into an invisible one.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T14:20:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T14:52:09Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/332"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/handover-purpose",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 82,
      "name": "Nothing links to HANDOVER.md and no item covers it, so it rotted where it was read",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/361",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "post-v1",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "HANDOVER.md says only true things, and the question of what the file IS has an answer written into the file itself: the owner's release runbook, or a historical note whose \"Before a public release\" checklist moves somewhere a person cutting a release would look.\n\nCLAUSE (a), THE FALSE STATEMENTS, IS DISCHARGED, 2026-09-21, in PR #363. Four \"What is not built\" rows named things that had shipped -- the three-pane shell (app/Livewire/Files/Browser.php), the admin surface (five screens under app/Livewire/Admin/), the REST API with Sanctum (app/Http/Controllers/Api/V1/, 16 paths in docs/api/openapi.json, contract-tested both directions) and the documentation site (docs/self-hosting/ plus pages.yml, build green and deploy skipping until item/docs-site's two settings). A \"Finish purging, or document that retention is manual\" checklist step was removed rather than corrected, because the same file refutes it two sections up under \"Purging is built\". Two rotted numbers -- \"99 commits, 386 tests\" against 795 and 898, and \"~1.34 GB\" against the README's ratcheted 934.2 MiB -- became pointers to where the numbers live instead of fresh copies that will rot again.\n\nCLAUSE (b), WHAT THE FILE IS FOR, IS OPEN and is the owner's, which is why this item is post-v1 rather than a v1 blocker: after (a) the tag procedure in step 2 is correct, so nothing about cutting v1.0.0 waits on the answer. Issue #361 stays open for it.\n\nTHE SPLIT IS THE POINT, and it is decision/0114's pattern at instance eight: the issue was filed owner-gated as a whole when only half of it was. What a file is FOR needs a decision; whether it says true things does not.",
      "actionStatus": "ActiveActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T19:15:12Z",
      "endTime": null,
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/363"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/release-announcement-gate",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 83,
      "name": "The Release is announced in parallel with two of the three jobs that verify the image",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/365",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "release.yml's `release` job waits for every job that verifies the published image, not one of them, and a dependency cannot skip out from under that wait.\n\nDISCHARGED 2026-09-21 in PR #366. decision/0103 (issue #303) established that a GitHub Release is the user-visible announcement and must not be published in parallel with the job proving the image it announces, and fixed ONE instance. verify-version-label and verify-latest-resolves-to-the-tag still hung off `image` alone. The second is the worse one: it exists because the README quick start is a bare `docker pull` with no tag, so a red leg there means the command in the README hands a self-hoster a different image from the one just announced.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT A ONE-LINE `needs:` EDIT, and this is the part worth keeping: verify-latest-resolves-to-the-tag was gated `&& !contains(github.ref_name, '-')`, narrower than `release`. A SKIPPED `needs` dependency propagates its skip to the dependent, so adding it unchanged would have meant no GitHub Release at all for any pre-release tag -- worse than the gap it closes. Measured rather than recalled, in https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35648660548: four jobs on a branch, `skipped-dependency` was `if: false`, the dependent with a plain `if:` naming something else was SKIPPED, and only the dependent written `!failure() && !cancelled()` ran. That second form was available and was rejected: it would also let `release` proceed when boot-published-image is skipped, which is the regression #303 fixed.\n\nSo the pre-release exclusion moved INSIDE the job, which now succeeds with nothing asserted on an rc -- the idiom changelog-section already uses for a non-tag ref -- and carries exactly the gate `release` carries. assert-release-gate.py gained both edges and a new rule: a dependency's `if:` must be ABSENT or STRING-IDENTICAL to the gated job's. String equality on purpose, because that script cannot evaluate a GitHub expression and a check that guessed at what two of them mean would be a guard whose answer is a guess.\n\nTHE COST IS STATED because it is real and is decision/0113's shape applied to a check NAME. On an rc, a check called \"a bare docker pull resolves to this tag\" goes green having asserted nothing. The green is honest only to a reader who opens the log. That is the trade for not skipping, and the alternative was worse.\n\nMUTATION, three mutants, each refused, control passed: dropping either new edge from release.needs, and restoring the narrow `if:`. The third is the one that matters -- it is the mistake this change was one edit away from shipping.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T20:07:35Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T20:12:58Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/366"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/loop-serialise-merges",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 84,
      "name": "A second merge cancels the first merge's main verification, and LOOP did not say to wait",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/370",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "docs/LOOP.md says that step 3 -- watch `main` after each merge -- must FINISH before step 2 merges again, and says what to do with a run that was cancelled anyway.\n\nDISCHARGED 2026-09-21 in PR #370, filed against an incident in the same iteration. Step 2 said merge and step 3 said watch; nothing said step 3 must complete first. Every workflow a `main` push triggers cancels in progress on a group keyed by workflow and ref (tests.yml and ledger.yml use `${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}`; pages.yml prefixes it with `pages-`), and every `main` push shares the ref, so a second merge while the first merge's run is still going cancels it.\n\nTHE INCIDENT: e2b9587 (PR #366) lost its `tests` run to the merge of PR #367 about two minutes later. It kept `ledger` and `pages`, both of which finished inside the gap, and lost the longest -- which is the general shape: the run most likely to be cancelled is always the one whose conclusion is worth the most. The run was RE-RUN to success as attempt 2 before the ledger recorded the merge, so this run's record for e2b9587 points at a real green run and not at an argument.\n\nWHY IT IS A DECISION AND NOT A SLIP: LOOP.md already described this exact cancellation, at ba799a9, and taught the loop how to READ the result -- workflow runs filtered to main, never check runs on the sha, because a cancelled run reports `cancelled` rather than looking green. That machinery worked perfectly: the watcher reported `tests completed cancelled` exactly as designed. It just reports after the damage. Knowing how to detect something is not the same as not doing it, and only the merge step can be the fix (decision/0128).\n\nTWO CLAIMS IN THE DRAFT WERE CHECKED AND BOTH WERE WRONG, which is why they are named here rather than smoothed over: pages.yml's concurrency group is `pages-${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}`, not the bare form the other two use; and `tests` was called \"the one that takes twenty minutes\" when the six most recent successful main runs of it took about three. The shipped text says \"longest\" and cites no number it did not measure.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T20:15:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T20:28:55Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/370"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/image-size-docs-agree",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 85,
      "name": "The README's image size and the enforced budget are two copies with no agreement check",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/368",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "README.md's Image size table and .github/image-budget.json cannot disagree without CI refusing.\n\nDISCHARGED 2026-09-21 in PR #369. The two files held the same three facts -- two byte counts and the commit they were measured at -- and image-size.sh gated only the budget file. What made it worse than an ordinary duplicate is that the SUPPORTED procedure broke it: the budget file's own note says raising a figure takes a Decision saying why, and doing exactly that left the README quoting a size this project no longer builds, in the file a self-hoster reads before pulling anything, with CI green throughout because the only gate was on the file that was updated. decision/0125.\n\nTHE MiB FIGURES WERE A SECOND, QUIETER COPY. image-size.sh derives 934.2 from 979591435 by integer division; the README's 934.2 was typed. Two numbers that happen to match are not one number (decision/0080), so the check REPRODUCES to_mib_1dp and compares against the result rather than comparing two strings both written by hand. The measuredAtCommit sha is COUNTED, not found: the table has a column per metric, so a README that updated one column and not the other still contains the sha once.\n\nIT MEASURES NOTHING. image-size.sh owns that against a real build in the `image` job, so this is a second-long text comparison in a job with no `needs:`, not a slower duplicate of a check that already exists. The PR check count went 22 -> 23.\n\nMUTATION, four mutants, each refused, control passed: the budget raised with the README left behind; the README's MiB edited off the derived value; one column's Measured-at-commit updated and not the other; and the \"## Image size\" heading renamed. The fourth is the load-bearing one -- without it every other assertion here would be vacuous the moment somebody restructured the README, and vacuous in the direction that reads as green (decision/0113).",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T20:13:27Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T20:24:16Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/369"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/runbook-trash-purge",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 86,
      "name": "The operations runbook describes trash purging backwards, and a directory purge that does not exist",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/373",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "docs/self-hosting/operations-runbook.md's Trash section says what PurgeFile does.\n\nDISCHARGED 2026-09-21 in PR #374. The section was ONE SENTENCE and every clause in it was wrong, on the page the docs site publishes for the operator performing the one irreversible operation in the product.\n\n(1) \"the same purge machinery as archived periods\" -- no; period purge is PeriodPurger from a console command, trash purge is app/Actions/Files/PurgeFile.php, which its own docblock calls \"the per-item complement\".\n\n(2) \"the retention/legal-hold guards above apply identically\" -- the legal-hold one does; the other is INVERTED. A period purge REQUIRES the period archived and past retention; PurgeFile REFUSES when the period is archived and has no retention check at all. So an operator who read this and archived the period first was told to do the exact thing that makes the purge refuse.\n\n(3) \"Soft-deleted DIRECTORIES and files ... permanently purges one item at a time\" -- there is no directory purge. Nine actions under app/Actions/Directories/ and no PurgeDirectory; `grep -rc PurgeDirectory app/` is 0 in every file; Livewire\\Trash\\Index says \"There is no directory purge here on purpose\".\n\nTHE REWRITE TAKES ITS REASONS FROM THE CODE rather than restating the rule, because both reasons are things an operator needs and neither reached them: an archived period is read-only and the sanctioned route is the retention-gated period purge, which a one-off delete would leave with inconsistent counts; and a directory purge is absent because files.directory_id is constrained()->cascadeOnDelete(), so force-deleting a directory row would delete every descendant file row at the database level without the application ever reaching object storage -- a storage leak, not a purge. It also says what to do instead, which the old text could not, because it did not know the operation it described was refused.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T20:36:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T20:49:14Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/374"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/merge-record-covers-main-push",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 87,
      "name": "Run.merges names two workflows; a main push triggers three",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/375",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "size": "M",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "Run.merges carries one field pair per workflow a push to main triggers, and something outside the prose refuses if the set drifts.\n\nDISCHARGED 2026-09-21 in PR #376. item/ledger-main-push-record chose named fields over a list and justified it: \"those are the whole of what a main push triggers, so a closed pair cannot under-report where a list is only as complete as its caller\". pages.yml is `on: push: branches: [main]` and was never in that enumeration, so the schema was a list-of-two wearing the authority of a complete set -- strictly worse than a list, which at least admits it is only as complete as its caller. A red pages build on main had nowhere to be recorded and was invisible to the rule that a completed run's last merge closed green. Practice had already diverged: run/0021's own description reads \"verified green per check name (tests 537, ledger 533, pages 27)\".\n\nA CLOSED SET CANNOT UNDER-REPORT ONLY IF THE SET IS RIGHT, and nothing checked that, because the set lived in prose -- LOOP.md, an item's doneWhen, and four hardcoded pairs in LedgerValidator. It is now one constant, MAIN_PUSH_WORKFLOWS, and .github/scripts/assert-merge-record-covers-main-push.py parses .github/workflows/ and refuses unless the two agree IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. LedgerValidator cannot do this itself: it never reads the workflows.\n\nBACKFILLED rather than gated behind an effective-after constant: all 262 entries across 21 run files carry the pair, real on the 73 merges at or after fa499f5 (the first-parent commit at which pages.yml existed), explicit null on the 189 before it. The boundary was computed, and the computation was rewritten once: the first version compared against a3a75c8, which is not on main's first-parent line, so it reported \"none missing\" for the wrong reason -- a guard that finds nothing and passes, which is the failure this project keeps meeting.\n\nTHE TAG-ONLY CASE is written into the script. A first draft read \"a push trigger with no `branches` key\" as \"every branch\" and so counted release.yml (`push: tags: [v*]`) -- precisely the workflow whose runs must never be recorded as a merge's verification, because no merge produces one.\n\nMUTATION. The guard: the constant back to the two it used to name -> exit 1; a new main-push workflow appears -> exit 1; pages stops triggering on main with the constant unchanged -> exit 1; the constant deleted -> exit 1; control -> exit 0. The extended validator rules, against a real fixture: pages red on the closing merge with tests and ledger green -> refused; a pagesConclusion with a null pagesRun -> refused.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T20:55:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T21:12:32Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/376"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/no-undefined-path-segment",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 88,
      "name": "A directory named `undefined` is tracked at the repo root and ships in the image",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/377",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "No tracked path has a segment that is a JavaScript accident, and CI refuses one.\n\nDISCHARGED 2026-09-21 in PR #378. `undefined/H1-header.png` -- 16,681 bytes, referenced by nothing -- was added by 7e78aa1 on 2026-09-18. .dockerignore has 14 entries and none covers it, and the Dockerfile is `COPY . .` three times, so it shipped in every published image layer, inside the bytes the image-size ratchet measures, under a directory whose name is a bug.\n\nTHE CLASS REFUSES, NOT THE FILE: a path segment named undefined, null, NaN or [object Object] is a tool interpolating a value it did not have, and nobody types one. Anchored on whole segments with awk -F/, not a substring, so a deliberate `undefined.test.ts` or a directory `not-undefined/` stays legal -- and THAT is the case that earns the design, because a check which fails legitimate work is a check somebody deletes.\n\nNOT .dockerignore, which was the obvious alternative and the wrong one: excluding the file from the image would leave it in the repository, which is the part that is actually wrong.\n\nMUTATION, four cases: control clean; the file restored -> refused; a nested resources/js/null/deep/a.ts -> refused; resources/js/not-undefined/undefined.test.ts -> clean.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T21:14:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T21:23:25Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/378"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/stale-texts-sweep",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 89,
      "name": "Four texts that assert something the tree contradicts",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/379",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "The four texts the consultation found asserting things the tree contradicts say what the tree says.\n\nDISCHARGED 2026-09-21 in PR #379, and two were worse than reported.\n\nupgrading.md justified \"downgrading is not supported\" with \"no migration in database/migrations/ is written with a working down() beyond what Laravel generates by default\". 24 of the 25 have a down() with a body, several hand-written. THE POLICY IS RIGHT AND THE REASON WAS FALSE, so the reason was replaced rather than the policy: exactly one migration cannot reverse (the email_verified_at backfill, which has no record of which rows it touched), and a down() that works still destroys data, because rolling back a column drop recreates the column empty. See decision/0130.\n\nconfiguration-reference.md contradicted itself on the same published page: a \"## Mail\" section saying the block \"is not optional\" for any instance with more than one person on it, and an \"## Everything else\" section listing \"the MAIL_* block\" among framework defaults the boot never asks you to set. Only four MAIL_* keys are exempt and DOC_AUDIT_ENV_KEY_EXEMPTIONS names them; MAIL_LOG_CHANNEL's own exemption text already said \"a debugging detail of the default that the Mail section tells you to move off\", so the registry knew what the prose did not.\n\nCONTRIBUTING.md listed 8 of 11 tests.yml jobs, and tests.yml's matrix comment said \"8.4 is what the image ships today\" while Dockerfile:12 says 8.5 and PRIMARY_PHP's own comment said it had drifted.\n\nNO GUARD FOR THE CONTRIBUTING LIST, deliberately and stated in the PR: it is the third \"two copies, no agreement check\" in one iteration, and the other two are numbers a self-hoster or the ledger depends on. A contributor-convenience list that names its own authority is not worth a fourth CI job.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-21T21:40:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-21T21:53:02Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/379"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/citation-addresses-rot",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 90,
      "name": "The ledger cites file:line into files that grow, so an insertion above a citation invalidates it silently",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/371",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "size": "M",
      "release": "post-v1",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "doneWhen": "Citations in the ledger and docs name something that moves WITH the thing it names -- a job, a heading, a function, a quoted fragment -- rather than a line number, or something checks the line numbers that remain.\n\nOPEN, and filed rather than swept, because changing the convention means auditing every existing citation. Found while correcting three ledger fields PR #366 falsified: three of the four rotted texts were behaviour claims, which are the change author's to sweep and were swept in that pass. The fourth is different and nobody was careless -- item/release-v0-1-0 cites \"release.yml:131\" for the `latest` rule, which is now line 207, moved by PR #359 inserting a job above it in a file it never opened. An insertion anywhere above a citation invalidates every citation below it, in files the inserting PR has no reason to read, and there is no moment at which anyone is in a position to notice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE HERE than in most repositories: decision/0080 makes a citation a claim that gets checked. A rotted line number defeats that at the cheapest step -- the reader opens the line, finds something unrelated, and has to decide whether the claim was wrong, the file moved, or they misread. The likely outcome is that they stop checking citations, which is the whole defence. See decision/0129.\n\nPARTIALLY DISCHARGED, 2026-09-21, and quantified rather than estimated. A sweep of every `path:line` citation in ledger.jsonld found 26 across 14 files. ALL TEN IN release.yml WERE WRONG -- eight are repointed to content anchors (a job name, a `tags:` entry, a quoted fragment), and the remaining two deliberately quote the stale address because they are the paragraphs that DESCRIBE the rot. They were wrong by seventy-odd lines, because #359 and others inserted jobs above them without opening this file.\n\nTHE OTHER SIXTEEN ARE NOT SWEPT and the survey of them is a SPOT-CHECK, said plainly because the temptation is to report the sweep as complete. Reading the cited line and comparing it with what the citation claims: some are stale (README.md:10 is now blank, Browser.php:239 is a bare `}`, browser.blade.php:223 is blank), some are exactly right (FirstRun.php:405 quotes its line verbatim, config/session.php:130 is the wrapped `env(` call), and some quote a state the tree has since changed on purpose (login.blade.php:18 was `type=\"email\"` and is `type=\"text\"` since login-by-username), which is history rather than rot. Telling those three apart needs each citation read against its own claim, which is the work this item still holds.\n\nA CAVEAT ON THE SURVEY ITSELF: two entries came back \"FILE NOT FOUND\" and neither was a finding -- build-docs-site.mjs and container-smoke.mjs live under .github/scripts/ and the sweep's glob missed them. A tool written to find rot produced two false positives on its first run, which is the reason the sixteen are reported as a spot-check and not as a verdict.\n\nTHE AUDIT IS DONE, 2026-09-22, and it changed what the right fix is -- which is why it was worth running rather than assuming. All 22 non-release.yml citations were read against the claim each one makes: 7 stale, 12 accurate, 1 historical-but-not-rot (decision/0031 recording login.blade.php:18 as `type=\"email\"`, which was true until login-by-username changed it to `type=\"text\"`), and 3 needing manual resolution -- TWO OF WHICH WERE THE SURVEY TOOL'S OWN BUG AGAIN, Python's glob skipping dot-directories, so .github/scripts/build-docs-site.mjs:52 read as \"not found\" when it is in fact exactly right (`export const INDEX_PAGE = { file: 'README.md', slug: 'index' };`).\n\nTHE FINDING THAT MATTERS: operative text and historical text need opposite treatment, and the ledger already says so without having drawn the consequence. decision/0083 put operative clauses in doneWhen precisely so a Decision's prose is not what anyone re-reads, and item/release-v0-1-0 records that the ledger is append-only for history. So a rotted citation inside a Decision's rationale is NOT a defect to repair -- decision/0031's `type=\"email\"` was true when it was written, and repointing it would falsify a dated record. Only an ITEM's doneWhen is read for what to do now, so only those citations are worth repointing. That collapses the work from \"7 stale citations\" to two: item/api-content and item/readme-owner, repointed here. The rest are classified and left standing.\n\nWHAT REMAINS is the convention itself -- a rule that new citations name an anchor that moves, and ideally something that checks it. The 26-citation corpus is now classified, so whoever builds that starts from a list rather than a survey.",
      "actionStatus": "PotentialActionStatus",
      "startTime": null,
      "endTime": null,
      "result": []
    },
