    {
      "@id": "item/directory-share-permission",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 120,
      "name": "A permission of its own for sharing a directory",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/36",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "post-v1",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/directory-access-ui"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "A directories.share permission gates DirectoryPolicy::manageAccess() and nothing else, so a member may hand out access to a subtree they hold Manage on without also gaining move and delete over it -- which is what granting directories.manage wholesale would do, and why this needs its own permission rather than a reused one. Both layers still apply; no layer is removed from manageAccess(). See decision/0036, which decided that sharing is an administrator act for v1.",
      "actionStatus": "PotentialActionStatus",
      "startTime": null,
      "endTime": null,
      "result": []
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/compose-boot-ci",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 121,
      "name": "CI boots the compose stack, not only the single container",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/36",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "size": "M",
      "release": "post-v1",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/compose-secret"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "CLAUDE.md calls `docker compose up -d --build` 'the real stack', and nothing in CI has ever started it. tests.yml's image job drives a single `docker run` container, which is the right primary target -- the container IS the product -- but it means every compose-only concern is unverified: the profiles, the healthchecks, the service dependency order, and whether the app container can actually reach a minio in a sibling service rather than the one supervisor runs inside it. item/compose-secret verifies compose statically with `docker compose config`, which proves interpolation and required variables and proves nothing about whether the thing runs. This item is the other half. Done when CI brings up the storage profile, waits for health, and asserts the app answers on its port with object storage served by the SIBLING minio -- and when a mutation shows it failing: point the app at a wrong bucket or drop the minio dependency and the job must go red. Deliberately post-v1: the single-container smoke covers the product, this covers a convenience, and it is not worth delaying a tag for.\n\nSPLIT AND HALF DISCHARGED, 2026-09-22, in PR #383. The clause has two independent halves and only one of them needed anyone's permission.\n\nHALF ONE IS BUILT: the `compose-boot` job brings up the DEFAULT stack (app, worker, worker-ingest, scheduler, sharing db:/data), waits for the app to serve the installer, and asserts the three non-app services reach `healthy` rather than merely `created`. On its first run the app answered 7.6 seconds after the containers started and all three workers reported healthy. PR check count 23 -> 24.\n\nHALF TWO IS NOT, and is not blocked on building: it is blocked on issue #382. The clause asks CI to assert \"object storage served by the SIBLING minio\", and NOTHING in compose.yaml produces that configuration -- AWS_ENDPOINT appears three times, all `http://app:9000`, and `app` keeps DOCCUM_EMBEDDED_STORAGE=true, so `--profile storage` starts a MinIO that no service reads. Asserting it in CI would mean asserting a configuration no documented path produces, which is worth less than it looks. #382 asks whether the profile should wire itself or stay a documented two-step; the answer decides what this half should assert.\n\nWHAT HALF ONE PROVES IS NARROWER THAN ITS NAME, stated here rather than left for a reader to discover. The three workers reported healthy about eight seconds in, before migrations could plausibly have finished -- only `app` carries AUTORUN_ENABLED=true, and the workers start `queue:work` against the same SQLite file without waiting. Their healthchecks are `pgrep`, which proves a PROCESS EXISTS and says nothing about whether it can reach the database. So \"worker is healthy\" is a weaker claim than it reads as, and a worker that started and could not query would satisfy it. That is a limit of compose.yaml's healthchecks, not of the job, and it is recorded because the alternative is a future reader treating a green compose-boot as proof the queue works.",
      "actionStatus": "ActiveActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-22T00:45:00Z",
      "endTime": null,
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/383"
      ]
    }
  ],
