      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/228",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "d76710119f75731716a9e3f6c34466d094b602dd",
      "mutant": "Made changelog-section.sh return empty and exit 0 on both failure paths -- no matching heading, and a heading whose body is blank -- instead of exiting non-zero. That is the silent defect the item was written against: release.yml would publish a GitHub Release with blank notes, successfully.",
      "check": "The changelog-section job in tests.yml. Run locally against the real script rather than in CI, because the extractor is a script and not an image, and re-run independently during review rather than taken from the implementing pass's report. MUTATED: the missing-version assertion got exit 0 with empty output where it requires non-zero, so the check fails. THE RESULT WORTH KEEPING is which half bit: under the same mutation the HAPPY-PATH assertion still passed. Only the missing-version case catches it. An extractor tested solely on the section that exists proves nothing about the case that will actually happen -- somebody tagging a version whose notes they forgot to write. Restored byte-identical afterwards, confirmed with cmp, and the restored script exits 1 on the missing version again.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0024",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/smoke-installer-names-the-bounce"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/238",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "4420606cd0dae26fb987e100504ee5abb5dce36b",
      "mutant": "Commented out app/Livewire/Setup/FirstRun.php:405's $user->forceFill(['email_verified_at' => now()])->save(); so the first administrator the installer creates is left unverified. That is exactly the regression item/email-verification-decided shipped against: with verification enforced and no mailer configured, an unverified first administrator is bounced to /email/verify and can never leave it, because the only way off that page is a mail the instance cannot send.",
      "check": "The image job's container smoke, run/35387325216. MUTATED: the installer step failed with the message this item added, quoted from the job log rather than inferred from the job's red status -- '[setup] the installer never reached /files after submitting the administrator form -- bounced to the email verification notice (/email/verify) instead of the page this was asked for. A bounce to the verification notice here means the first administrator was not verified at creation (item/email-verification-decided, issue #161); a bounce anywhere else (e.g. back to \\'/\\', Laravel\\'s starter welcome page) is issue #97\\'s original failure mode instead.' The distinguishing half is what was on trial and the distinguishing half is what fired: the smoke named the verification bounce specifically, not a generic failure to arrive. WHAT THIS MUTATION DOES NOT PROVE, recorded because it weakens it: the same mutant also turned all six test jobs red, so this is not a case where the smoke was the only thing standing between the defect and a green tree. The evidence here is the message text read out of the image job's own log, not the redness of the run -- a red run would have been consistent with the old bare-boolean message too, and that is the thing this item replaced. Closed unmerged; main never carried the mutant.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0025",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/directory-force-delete-orphans"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/241",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "2f9a1ea49bc6217b9465ee0bc013a9da4f8377f0",
      "mutant": "Removed Directory::booted()'s forceDeleting hook and its import -- literally the code as it stood before this item. Removed WHOLE rather than commented out, deliberately: a commented-out body leaves the import unused, pint fails, and a mutation PR that goes red on formatting says nothing about the guard.",
      "check": "The six test jobs, run/35389348140. MUTATED: 'Tests: 2 failed, 1 skipped, 861 passed (1918 assertions)'. Two, not four and not six, and they are the two named in the PR before the run. The object test reported its survivor list in full -- ['root','child','trashed','trashedChild'] -- so all four objects outlived the force-delete, which is issue #86's orphaning reproduced exactly. Two of those four are the independently-trashed cases, the file soft-deleted on its own and the file inside a soft-deleted child directory: the rows Directory::descendants() drops through the SoftDeletes scope and the cascade removes anyway, which is what withTrashed() is in the action for. The collected-list form is what made them visible; a per-key toBeFalse() would have reported 'root' and stopped. THE RESULT WORTH KEEPING is what stayed green: 'it leaves a sibling directory and its object untouched' PASSED, as did SchemaPortabilityTest. That test asserts an untouched directory keeps its object, which the broken code satisfies too -- had it reddened, the suite would have been measuring something wider than the subtree and the other two failures would have meant less. Closed unmerged; main never carried the mutant.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0026",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/image-size"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/246",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "f5bc189911f5f2ed67adcbb689e5f388f8ddd5c1",
      "mutant": "A 50 MB (52428800 byte) INCOMPRESSIBLE layer added to the Dockerfile's app stage: head -c 52428800 /dev/urandom > /mutation-fat.bin. Grown rather than shrinking the recorded figure, which was the other candidate and was rejected twice over: with a 2% tolerance a one-byte edit to the budget sits inside the noise and fires nothing, and more fundamentally, editing the budget file tests the script's arithmetic while growing the image tests measurement and comparison together -- and growth is the failure the ratchet exists to catch.",
      "check": "The image job in tests.yml, run/35391439074. MUTATED: image was the ONLY failing check, 14 of 15 green, which is the half of the prediction that had to hold -- nothing in this mutant touches PHP, JS or the ledger, so a red test job would have meant it reached further than intended. Both error lines fired and the step exited 1: uncompressed 1032004830 over the 999183263 ceiling by 32821567, compressed 360853640 over the 314606870 ceiling by 46246770. THE RESULT WORTH KEEPING is why /dev/urandom and not /dev/zero. Measured against the feature branch's own second build, the mutant grew 52413342 bytes uncompressed and 52418755 compressed -- essentially 100% of the layer survived gzip. Zeroes would have compressed to almost nothing: the uncompressed error still fires, the run is still red, and the compressed half of the gate goes ENTIRELY untested while looking proven. The two runs are indistinguishable from outside. Same trap as the topbar assertion in CLAUDE.md, where the half argued to carry the weight carried none. Closed unmerged; main never carried the layer.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0027",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/upgrade-smoke"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/251",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "7581911eab71664e5a8f265261a353d7f7150204",
      "mutant": "Removed the UPDATE from database/migrations/2026_09_18_150000_backfill_email_verified_at_for_existing_users.php's up(), leaving the migration present and still recording itself as run -- the fix gone, not the migration. Its now-unused DB import went with it so pint could not fail the PR for a reason unrelated to the assertion.",
      "check": "run/35395221353. INSIDE THE SMOKE the mutant was precise: checkStaleUserReachesTheApp failed with 'the user seeded with email_verified_at NULL before the replacement is bounced to the email verification notice (/email/verify) ... the backfill migration did not reach this row across the replacement, so it is stranded exactly the way issue #210 describes', while checkStaleRolePermissionStaysRevoked next to it PASSED -- 'member still lacks files.restore after the container replacement -- the edit survived -- OK'.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT SHOW, recorded because the board overstates it. All six test jobs also went red, through tests/Feature/BackfillEmailVerifiedAtMigrationTest.php, which inserts a raw row bypassing the factory and invokes the migration's own up() against it. The prediction written into the mutation PR before the run said every test job would stay green because no suite test asserts on this migration -- that was FALSE, taken from the implementing agent's report and never checked, where one grep over tests/ would have settled it. So this run does not show the smoke is what stands between the defect and a green tree; citing the red board as proof of the smoke's value counts one defect twice.\n\nThe smoke's MARGINAL guarantee over that unit test, which is what it actually earned here: the unit test proves the migration's SQL works when called directly; the smoke proves it EXECUTES during a real container replacement, against a row predating the image. Isolating that alone would need a mutation to boot-time migration, which cannot be done narrowly -- skipping migrations at boot breaks the first boot and fails everything.\n\nThe run also found a defect in the new code that only a firing diagnostic could show: the message read 'instead of the page this was asked for instead of /dashboard', because describeLanding() already supplies the first clause. Fixed in bed6912 before merge. Closed unmerged.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0028",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/directory-policy-trashed-update"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/254",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "66befe0d34bd8a0d5d1fbdc9b3d5d1384c3a7231",
      "mutant": "DirectoryPolicy::update()'s trashed guard deleted -- BY CI, not by a branch. This is the first mutation in this ledger with no mutation PR, and the absence is the finding: .github/mutations.json entry directory-policy-update-refuses-trashed-directory names the guard text and the test, and the guards job runs mutation-check.php, which removes the text, runs the named test, requires it to FAIL, and restores it. Registering a guard IS the mutation here.",
      "check": "The guards job on PR #254's own green run, quoted from its log:\n  guard present  -> \"refuses to update a trashed directory, even with access held through a grant above it\" passes\n  guard deleted  -> \"refuses to update a trashed directory, even with access held through a grant above it\" fails, as it must\n\nWHAT NEARLY HAPPENED, recorded because it is the reusable part. I had the mutation branch cut and the guard removed via the registry's own remove string before checking what the guards job actually runs. That branch would have proven nothing already proven, and would have turned guards RED for a structural reason: mutation-check.php requires the remove text to match exactly once, and with the guard already deleted it matches zero times. A mutation PR that fails on the registry rather than on the assertion is the same worthless red as one that fails on pint.\n\nSo the cost of mutation-checking a guard expressible as a contiguous block of code is one mutations.json entry, not a PR and a CI cycle. The mutation-PR workflow remains necessary for what the registry cannot express: smoke assertions (the guard is a container behaviour, not a line to delete) and anything whose removal must be observed in the image job.\n\nThe test itself is discriminating rather than merely passing: the grant sits on the PARENT and it asserts can('update', $child) true BEFORE trashing and false after, so a refusal cannot be explained by the user never having had access.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0029",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/directory-policy-trashed-move"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/256",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "68862569c405b14228f9395d2917a04ce4f890db",
      "mutant": "Both move() guards, each removed by the guards job from its own .github/mutations.json entry -- directory-policy-move-refuses-trashed-directory and directory-policy-move-refuses-trashed-destination. Second use of the registry-as-mutation mechanism recorded in mutation/0028, and the first with TWO independent guards in one file: each entry's remove string was verified to match app/Policies/DirectoryPolicy.php exactly once, so removing either leaves the other intact and the two verdicts are separate.",
      "check": "PR #256's own green guards job, quoted:\n  move-refuses-trashed-directory   guard present -> \"refuses to move a trashed directory, even with access held through a grant above it\" passes; guard deleted -> fails, as it must\n  move-refuses-trashed-destination guard present -> \"refuses to move a directory into a trashed destination, even with edit access granted on it\" passes; guard deleted -> fails, as it must\n\nBoth tests assert access TRUE before trashing and false after, with the grant above the subject: without the before-assertion a refusal is consistent with the user never having had access.\n\nWhat these prove and what they do not. They prove each guard is load-bearing for its own test. They do NOT prove the $newParent guard prevents the corruption it was written against -- no test asserts on the path column after a move into a trashed parent, because the guard now refuses that move before syncPath() ever runs. The corruption claim rests on reading Directory.php:113-117 against SoftDeletes at line 42, and is recorded as a reading, not a measurement.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0030",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/release-gate"
      ],
      "pullRequest": null,
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "94138e6f0a13396e3194bb11e3ea246b5a5f276f",
      "mutant": "A commit deliberately NOT on main: branch mutation/0030-release-gate-ancestry, cut from main at 9ad02bd (so it carries release.yml as merged in PR #231, merge 8fdfb50, verified an ancestor of main) plus one commit adding docs/mutations/0030-release-gate-ancestry.md. `git merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD origin/main` was run before pushing and answered no -- measured, not assumed.\n\nNOT A TAG. decision/0085 recorded that pushing a tag returns HTTP 403 here and treated that as blocking this item's mutation entirely. That was too wide: release.yml fires on `workflow_dispatch` as well as `push: tags`, and a dispatch against a branch was accepted (204). The tag wall blocks tags, not this proof. decision/0092 records the reasoning, including why a dispatch is nearer the reported defect than a tag would have been: the defect in issue #32 was that a DISPATCH against a red main built and pushed `latest`.\n\nSafe by construction, checked before firing rather than after: the only job that builds anything and the only job holding `packages: write` is `image`, and it sits two `needs:` edges downstream of `ancestry-check` (release.yml:66, 73). Nothing on this path can publish even if the gate had failed to fire.",
      "check": "Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35430359198 -- run_number 1, the first time release.yml has ever executed (decision/0075 predicted this item would be its first run). Job conclusions, read from the API rather than the summary page:\n\n  tagged commit is on main   FAILURE   at step 'Refuse to build/publish a commit that is not on main'\n  verify (tests.yml)         skipped\n  image                      skipped\n  release                    skipped\n  release-skipped-notice     skipped\n\nThe failing step's log, verbatim:\n\n  ##[error]94138e6f0a13396e3194bb11e3ea246b5a5f276f (ref refs/heads/mutation/0030-release-gate-ancestry) is not an ancestor of origin/main -- refusing to verify, build or publish. Only tag or dispatch a commit that has already been merged to main.\n  ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.\n\nElapsed: 5 seconds, which is the point of placing ancestry-check before verify rather than only before image.\n\nWHAT THIS PROVES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT, stated separately because decision/0080 exists.\n\nProven by observation: the ancestry-check refusal fires on a real dispatch of release.yml against a real commit that was never merged, and nothing downstream runs. Also observed: the needs-chain propagates a non-success -- verify did not run because ancestry-check did not succeed, and image did not run because verify did not succeed.\n\nNOT proven by observation: the specific state where ancestry-check passes and `verify` RUNS AND FAILS. That needs a commit which is on main, carries the gate, and is red. No such commit exists -- 40 completed tests.yml runs on main were listed and the only non-successes are two `cancelled` concurrency runs (8fdfb50, 60b72e8), zero failures. Creating one would mean pushing a red main, which is the thing this gate exists to make harmless. decision/0090 reached the same conclusion for tags; a dispatch does not change it. That the two cases are the same GitHub `needs:` evaluation is a reading of the documented semantics, not a measurement, and is recorded as a reading.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0031",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/api-sanctum-tokens"
      ],
      "pullRequest": null,
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "15dfc4a600ea58137b0918e6d8b8ed5db8c71a61",
      "mutant": "Branch mutation/0031-api-tokens-smoke: PR #262's smoke check plus one deliberate regression, shaped like something somebody would actually write -- stash the plaintext token in the session on create, restore it in a new mount(), because \"the operator loses the token when the page reloads\". Never merged; PR #263 closed unmerged. pullRequest is null deliberately: #263 is not a PullRequest node in this ledger and must not become one, because every registered PR here is part of the product's history and this branch is the opposite -- a tree built to be refused. Its run URL is in `check`, which is where the evidence belongs.\n\nThe CHOICE of mutant is the substance here. Breaking creation outright would have proved only that the check runs at all. CLAUDE.md's account-menu lesson is that the obvious half of an assertion is often the worthless one and that reasoning about which half carries weight is unreliable -- the topbar check's hidden-before half was argued load-bearing and mutation proved the reverse. So this mutant leaves creation working and breaks only \"shown exactly once\", isolating the reload assertion, which is the clause this item is actually about.",
      "check": "Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35435327343, image job. Verbatim, timestamps stripped:\n\n  [setup] opening /settings/api-tokens as the administrator\n  [setup] /settings/api-tokens bounced to the password-confirm gate -- confirming with the admin's password\n  [setup] password-confirm gate cleared, back at /settings/api-tokens -- OK\n  [setup] creating a token named \"Smoke Token 811011512\" through the real form\n  [setup] the plaintext token appeared on the page right after creating it -- OK\n  [setup] reloading /settings/api-tokens and checking that exact plaintext value is gone\n  ##[error][setup] the plaintext token captured off \"Smoke Token 811011512\"'s creation is still on the page after a reload of /settings/api-tokens\n  ##[error]container-smoke (setup) failed: plaintext token value survived a reload of /settings/api-tokens\n  ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.\n\nTHREE THINGS, established separately rather than pooled.\n\n(1) The mutation was ISOLATED. \"the plaintext token appeared on the page right after creating it -- OK\" is present, so creation still worked and only the reload assertion failed. Had the check died earlier the reload assertion would still be unproven and PR #262 would have gone back for rework rather than the item closing -- which is precisely the outcome this run was built to be able to produce.\n\n(2) The password-confirm path RAN. The gate fired and was cleared; the \"no password-confirm gate this time\" branch never executed. This smoke had never driven a password.confirm interstitial before, so that path is exercised rather than assumed.\n\n(3) The other half. Against the same commit with the feature intact, PR #262 was green on all 16 checks including image. Check passes with the feature, fails without it -- both halves observed, neither inferred from the other.\n\nONE CORRECTION to this session's own framing, recorded rather than quietly dropped: PR #263's body said a dumpContainerState header would precede the error. The literal string appears nowhere in the log, and the container dump (--- docker logs ---) follows the thrown error rather than preceding it. The diagnostic is real and adjacent; its position was described wrongly.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0032",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/palette-smoke"
      ],
      "pullRequest": null,
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "dec5a900005c847fba712e4865e2b94a80038998",
      "mutant": "Branch mutation/0032-palette-smoke: PR #277's palette smoke check plus one deliberate regression -- remove exactly one attribute, x-on:keydown.enter.prevent, from the palette input. Never merged; PR #278 closed unmerged. pullRequest is null deliberately, on the same grounds as mutation/0031: every registered PullRequest here is part of the product's history and this branch is the opposite, a tree built to be refused. Its run URLs are in `check`.\n\nTHE CHOICE OF MUTANT IS THE SUBSTANCE, and here the obvious mutant would have been worthless rather than merely weak. Ctrl-K is handled TWICE and INDEPENDENTLY: the topbar's focusSearch() (layouts/app/topbar.blade.php) dispatches an open-search-palette CustomEvent, and the palette's own x-on:keydown.window.prevent.ctrl.k calls $wire.openPalette() directly. Deleting either one alone leaves the keystroke working through the other, so a Ctrl-K mutant would have gone GREEN and been recorded as proof. That is decision/0080's \"counting readers is not counting sources\" arriving on the writing side: two writers of one behaviour, neither a witness to itself.\n\nThe Enter mutant was chosen instead for three reasons, each checkable rather than argued. RELIABLE: <livewire:search.palette /> is mounted outside the topbar's GET form (topbar.blade.php line 326, after </flux:main>), so the palette input sits in no form at all and Enter with the handler gone is inert rather than subtly different -- a mutant that might still work proves nothing and costs a full image build to discover. ISOLATING: it touches nothing the three earlier assertions depend on, so they must still pass in the failing run. UNREACHABLE OTHERWISE: openSelected() and the selected state live entirely in the root element's x-data, so the test renderer cannot execute a line of it and PaletteTest.php never tries.",
      "check": "BOTH DIRECTIONS, on two runs of the same check.\n\nDIRECTION 2, the guard deleted. Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35468822002, image job, head dec5a90. Verbatim, timestamps stripped:\n\n  [setup] clearing the period filter restores smoke-35468822002-1.txt -- OK\n  [setup] opening the command palette with Ctrl-K from /files, searching in it, and opening a hit with Enter\n  [setup] Ctrl-K opened the command palette -- the window binding and its Livewire round trip both ran\n  [setup] the palette's debounced live query found smoke-35468822002-1.txt by a word inside it\n  [setup] the hit's snippet highlights smokeword354688220021 itself -- the escape-then-swap ran in the image\n  ##[error][setup] Enter on the palette did not navigate to the selected hit -- the URL stayed at http://127.0.0.1:8080/files, so Alpine's openSelected() did not reach the selected anchor\n  ##[error]container-smoke (setup) failed: Enter did not open the selected palette hit\n  ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.\n\nDIRECTION 1, the guard in place. Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35468807853, image job, SUCCESS, all eighteen checks green on PR #277:\n\n  [setup] Ctrl-K opened the command palette -- the window binding and its Livewire round trip both ran\n  [setup] the palette's debounced live query found smoke-35468807853-1.txt by a word inside it\n  [setup] the hit's snippet highlights smokeword354688078531 itself -- the escape-then-swap ran in the image\n  [setup] Enter opened the selected hit at /files/1?file=1 -- OK\n\nTHREE THINGS, established separately rather than pooled.\n\n(1) LOAD-BEARING. The same check passes at 66b3bd5 and fails at dec5a90, and the only difference between those trees is one deleted attribute.\n\n(2) ISOLATED. The three lines before the failure printed IN THE FAILING RUN, so Ctrl-K still opened the palette, the live query still reached Services\\Search, and the snippet still rendered a <mark> with the mutated attribute gone. Those three do not depend on it, and the failure is attributable to the one clause the mutant touched rather than to the palette having stopped working generally.\n\n(3) READS IN THE RIGHT DIRECTION. The failure names the assertion and the observed state -- \"the URL stayed at http://127.0.0.1:8080/files\" -- rather than surfacing as a bare waitForURL timeout. A failure that names no assertion is one nobody can act on.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH, recorded here so that a reader of the green log cannot infer otherwise. Exactly ONE of the check's assertions is mutation-proven. The other three passed in BOTH directions, which is what makes them a control for (2) and precisely why they are not evidence about themselves. Nothing here says the Ctrl-K assertion would fail if the palette's keyboard wiring broke; only that it is unaffected by THIS mutant. The two assertions PR #280 added -- the Alpine-boot precondition and the topbar-focus leg -- are not mutation-proven either, and are labelled as such in the code rather than folded into this count.\n\nNOTE ON THE LOG LINES QUOTED ABOVE. Both runs print \"the escape-then-swap ran in the image\". That wording overstated what the assertion proves, and PR #280 corrected it to name the swap only; the ESCAPE half is unproven and is issue #279. The lines are quoted here as they were EMITTED rather than as the code now reads, because a mutation record that edits its own evidence is not a record.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0033",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/release-v0-1-0"
      ],
      "pullRequest": null,
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "6ad7846598c8be71550bf1a965dde1fc6c4b1332",
      "mutant": "RE-RUN of mutation/0023, obliged by item/release-v0-1-0's own clause: \"Because the guard changes, mutation/0023 is RE-RUN, not assumed to still hold.\" Same mutant as 0023 -- changelog-section.sh's two failure paths (no such heading; heading present but body blank) return empty and exit 0 instead of exiting non-zero -- applied to the widened script that now falls back from a pre-release to its release section.\n\nRun locally against the real script and restored byte-identical afterwards, confirmed with cmp, the same way mutation/0023 was run: bash executes in this environment even though PHP does not, so this is the rare guard here that needs no CI round trip.",
      "check": "The four CI assertions from tests.yml's changelog-section job, extracted into a harness that calls the real script.\n\nBASELINE, guard in place: A1 pass, A2 pass, A3 pass, A4 pass.\n\nMUTATED:\n  A1 pass   (existing version extracts the [0.1.0] section)\n  A2 FAIL   exited 0 with empty output where non-zero is required\n  A3 pass   (pre-release falls back to [0.1.0])\n  A4 FAIL   exited 0 with empty output where non-zero is required\n\nTHE GUARD SURVIVES THE WIDENING, which is the question this re-run was for. Both happy paths still pass under the mutant and both missing-version paths still fail it -- the exact asymmetry mutation/0023 recorded (\"an extractor tested solely on the section that exists proves nothing about the case that will actually happen\"), now holding for the suffixed case too. A4 is the new one: without it, adding the fallback would have re-opened the blank-Release hole for every tag carrying a suffix, which is the shape this repository is about to start using.\n\nRestored, and the restored script passes all four again.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0034",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/release-v0-1-0"
      ],
      "pullRequest": null,
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "6ad7846598c8be71550bf1a965dde1fc6c4b1332",
      "mutant": "The pre-release fallback resolves to the WRONG section: `extract_section \"$core\"` becomes `extract_section \"Unreleased\"`. Everything else untouched, so the exact-match path and the no-suffix path are unaffected by construction.\n\nChosen to test the fallback's DESTINATION rather than its existence. Deleting the fallback outright would have failed both new assertions at once and proved only that they depend on it being there; this asks the sharper question -- does anything notice if the fallback fires but lands somewhere else?\n\nRun locally, restored byte-identical, confirmed with cmp.",
      "check": "Same harness as mutation/0033.\n\nMUTATED:\n  A1 pass\n  A2 pass\n  A3 FAIL   exit=1\n  A4 pass\n\nISOLATED, which is what the mutant was shaped for: only the pre-release assertion moved, so the failure is attributable to the fallback's destination and not to the script having broken generally.\n\nAND IT FAILED ON THE CLAUSE I DID NOT PREDICT. I expected the body comparison to catch this -- the assertion compares the rc output against the [0.1.0] output, and [Unreleased] is a different section. It never got that far: [Unreleased] in CHANGELOG.md is EMPTY, so the mutated fallback returned status 4 and the script exited 1, and the assertion failed on the exit code instead.\n\nSo this mutation says nothing whatever about whether the body comparison is load-bearing. It proves the assertion catches a fallback pointing at an empty section, and no more. mutation/0035 exists because of that, and this is CLAUDE.md's account-menu lesson arriving a third time in this project: reasoning about which half of an assertion carries the weight is unreliable, and the only way to know is to make each half fail on purpose.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0035",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/release-v0-1-0"
      ],
      "pullRequest": null,
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "6ad7846598c8be71550bf1a965dde1fc6c4b1332",
      "mutant": "The pre-release fallback finds the RIGHT section and returns a truncated body: `extract_section \"$core\"` becomes `extract_section \"$core\" | head -1`.\n\nWritten specifically because mutation/0034 failed on the exit code and therefore left the body comparison unproven. This mutant keeps the fallback succeeding -- right section, exit 0 -- and changes only what it returns, so the exit-code clause cannot fire and the body comparison is the only thing left that can catch it.\n\nRun locally, restored byte-identical, confirmed with cmp.",
      "check": "Same harness as mutation/0033 and 0034.\n\nMUTATED:\n  A1 pass\n  A2 pass\n  A3 FAIL   body differs from [0.1.0]\n  A4 pass\n\nTHE BODY COMPARISON IS LOAD-BEARING, which mutation/0034 could not establish. The failure names the clause -- \"body differs from [0.1.0]\" -- rather than an exit code, so the two mutants together partition the assertion: 0034 proves the exit-code clause catches a fallback that lands on an empty section, 0035 proves the body clause catches one that lands on the right section and returns the wrong text. An assertion asserting only `exit 0` would have passed this mutant and published a one-line Release.\n\nWHAT THE PAIR STILL DOES NOT ESTABLISH. Neither mutant makes the fallback land on a wrong section that is NON-empty, because CHANGELOG.md currently has only two sections and one of them is blank. The body comparison is proven against truncation, not against substitution. A changelog with a second populated section would allow that case; recorded rather than left for a reader to assume the partition is complete.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0036",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/snippet-escape-order"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/284",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "c4ddc7e41bddbca766e607f53dbeef8e0dd19881",
      "mutant": ".github/mutations.json entry `search-results-snippet-escaped-before-mark-swap`. Unlike most Mutation nodes here, this one is not a branch somebody pushed once: it is a registered guard that CI's `guards` job re-runs on every push, the same shape as mutation/0028 and mutation/0029.\n\nTHE MUTANT removes the bare letter `e` immediately before `(Str::limit($hit->snippet, 260))` in resources/views/livewire/search/results.blade.php, leaving a no-op parenthesised group. The `remove` string carries the preceding newline and indentation so that it matches exactly once -- verified with substr_count and cross-checked with grep -c, not by reading.\n\nWHY THAT MUTANT AND NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE. Deleting the whole e(...) call leaves str_replace() with two arguments: an ArgumentCountError that crashes the render. That would prove the test notices SOMETHING, not that it notices THIS. The bare-letter mutant is precise because the swap array is [e(MARK_OPEN), e(MARK_CLOSE)] and e() leaves a private-use codepoint untouched -- so under the mutant the marker swap still runs and produces a real <mark>, and the only thing lost is the escaping. The failure is therefore attributable to the ordering rather than to the view having broken.",
      "check": "The `guards` job on PR #284, run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35474841420, head c4ddc7e. Verbatim:\n\n  === search-results-snippet-escaped-before-mark-swap ===\n      guard present  -> \"escapes hostile markup in the results page snippet before swapping the match into a mark element\" passes\n      guard deleted  -> \"escapes hostile markup in the results page snippet before swapping the match into a mark element\" fails, as it must\n\nBOTH DIRECTIONS IN ONE RUN, which is what a registered entry buys over a hand-pushed mutation branch: mutation-check.php asserts that the named test passes with the guard in place AND fails with it deleted, then restores the file and the job asserts the working tree is clean.\n\nREAD FROM THE LOG, NOT INFERRED FROM THE JOB. A green `guards` says all registered guards held; it does not by itself say this entry ran at all -- an entry whose `remove` failed to match could in principle be skipped. The lines above were grepped out of the job log for that reason, and the run also printed \"All 94 guard(s) are load-bearing.\"",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
