      "mutant": ".github/mutations.json entry `search-palette-snippet-escaped-before-mark-swap`. Unlike most Mutation nodes here, this one is not a branch somebody pushed once: it is a registered guard that CI's `guards` job re-runs on every push, the same shape as mutation/0028 and mutation/0029.\n\nTHE MUTANT removes the bare letter `e` immediately before `(Str::limit($hit->snippet, 160))` in resources/views/livewire/search/palette.blade.php, leaving a no-op parenthesised group. The `remove` string carries the preceding newline and indentation so that it matches exactly once -- verified with substr_count and cross-checked with grep -c, not by reading.\n\nWHY THAT MUTANT AND NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE. Deleting the whole e(...) call leaves str_replace() with two arguments: an ArgumentCountError that crashes the render. That would prove the test notices SOMETHING, not that it notices THIS. The bare-letter mutant is precise because the swap array is [e(MARK_OPEN), e(MARK_CLOSE)] and e() leaves a private-use codepoint untouched -- so under the mutant the marker swap still runs and produces a real <mark>, and the only thing lost is the escaping. The failure is therefore attributable to the ordering rather than to the view having broken.",
      "check": "The `guards` job on PR #284, run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35474841420, head c4ddc7e. Verbatim:\n\n  === search-palette-snippet-escaped-before-mark-swap ===\n      guard present  -> \"escapes hostile markup in the palette snippet before swapping the match into a mark element\" passes\n      guard deleted  -> \"escapes hostile markup in the palette snippet before swapping the match into a mark element\" fails, as it must\n\nBOTH DIRECTIONS IN ONE RUN, which is what a registered entry buys over a hand-pushed mutation branch: mutation-check.php asserts that the named test passes with the guard in place AND fails with it deleted, then restores the file and the job asserts the working tree is clean.\n\nREAD FROM THE LOG, NOT INFERRED FROM THE JOB. A green `guards` says all registered guards held; it does not by itself say this entry ran at all -- an entry whose `remove` failed to match could in principle be skipped. The lines above were grepped out of the job log for that reason, and the run also printed \"All 94 guard(s) are load-bearing.\"",
