    {
      "@id": "mutation/0038",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/reach-existence-oracle"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/305",
      "run": "run/0021",
      "headSha": "db2e28fb707680ecb9778124369f7973cf130e0a",
      "mutant": ".github/mutations.json entry `drop-move-destination-scoped-to-reach`. A registered guard CI's `guards` job re-runs on every push, not a branch pushed once.\n\nTHE MUTANT removes only the line `->whereIn('id', $reachableForDrop)` from dropMove(), leaving Directory::query()->find($targetDirectoryId) -- a call that still parses and still runs. A destination that EXISTS outside the viewer's reach is then found, so abort_if($destination === null, 404) does not fire and authorize('move', ...) refuses with 403 instead. That difference IS the existence oracle: 403 for a directory that exists, 404 for an id that does not. The remove string was checked with substr_count against the source before pushing and matches exactly once.\n\nWHICH HALF OF THE PAIR THIS PROVES, stated because reasoning about it is unreliable (CLAUDE.md's account-menu lesson). Only the OUT-OF-REACH test flips. The companion test 'answers a destination that does not exist the same way as one out of reach' STILL PASSES with the whereIn removed, because find() returns null for a nonexistent id either way and abort_if still fires. It documents that the two answers agree; it is not this guard's witness, and expectFailing does not name it.",
      "check": "The `guards` job on PR #305, run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35580456926, head db2e28f. Verbatim:\n\n  === drop-move-destination-scoped-to-reach ===\n      guard present  -> \"refuses a drop into a directory the viewer cannot reach, and moves nothing\" passes\n      guard deleted  -> \"refuses a drop into a directory the viewer cannot reach, and moves nothing\" fails, as it must\n\nand the run's closing line, \"All 95 guard(s) are load-bearing.\"\n\nREAD FROM THE LOG, NOT INFERRED FROM THE JOB, and this run is why that distinction is not pedantry. The FIRST push of this PR (head a4452a8) had a GREEN result for this very entry and the job was still RED -- because a different, pre-existing entry had lost its witness to the same change. A green job would have been read as 'my mutation is proven'; it was, and something else was broken anyway. See decision/0111.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
