    {
      "@id": "mutation/0043",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/reach-existence-oracle"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/308",
      "run": "run/0021",
      "headSha": "4fa52d779c75a506716300534beb8479fda28cdb",
      "mutant": ".github/mutations.json entry `select-directory-root-scoped-to-reach`, a registered guard the CI `guards` job re-runs on every push. Removing the whereIn leaves Directory::query()->find($directoryId) unscoped at the root branch, so an unviewable root is found and answers 403 from authorize('view') while a nonexistent id answers 404.\n\nWHAT THE OLD UNION CONTRIBUTED, which is the finding this entry preserves. The branch used to union whereNull('parent_id') with reachRootIds(), and its comment defended that as \"the lookup only ever widens what can be found\" -- true, and silent about WHAT. reachRootIds() is derived from viewableDirectoryIds(), returning the viewable directories whose parent is null or not viewable, so every root a viewer may legitimately see is already in it, including every root for an admin holding directories.view-all. The disjunct added exactly one class of row: true roots the viewer CANNOT view. It widened the lookup by precisely the set that must be refused.\n\nWHICH HALF OF THE PAIR IT PROVES: only the out-of-reach test flips; the companion asserting a nonexistent id answers the same still passes with the scoping removed (decision/0111).",
      "check": "The `guards` job on PR #308, run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35584117401, head 4fa52d7. Verbatim:\n\n  === select-directory-root-scoped-to-reach ===\n      guard present  -> \"refuses to select a directory the viewer cannot view\" passes\n      guard deleted  -> \"refuses to select a directory the viewer cannot view\" fails, as it must\n\nand the closing line, \"All 100 guard(s) are load-bearing.\" The full six-way test matrix was also green, which is where the OTHER risk of this change would have surfaced: dropping a disjunct could have made a legitimately viewable root stop resolving, a functional regression rather than a security one.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0044",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/reach-oracle-route-binding"
